shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | `status: OK` measures attempted jobs only; cross-check `okCount` for SSD capture, and the expected count is not a constant — it moved 28→76 the same day | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Tracking session transcripts added ~368 MB to `~/.claude` and made repo size grow daily; left as an open decision because Tier B capture is a proven reconstruction fallback | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] | Multi-task Mac+Windows skills/backup plan: 42 own vs 19 third-party skills reframing, Task 4 inversion script deleted, Task 2 prune 306→208 as a reversible move, three open round-10 findings | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] | A `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matches directories but not symlinks, so the rule silently no-ops and an automated backup re-adds the entries; plus `.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` scope | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] | Restore test returned 57 of 61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`; only an actual restore can measure coverage the backup's own records cannot | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] | A rate-limit-forced switch from Codex to Claude found the plan's biggest error; resume cannot cross providers, and a prior approval is stale after an amendment (5 new bugs in the amendment itself) | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] | Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file, failing only while the SSD is mounted; fixed by rename, `okCount: 80` | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | rsync's refused type change, a directory-only ignore pattern skipping symlinks, and a one-hop link audit are the same defect: tooling classifies by type where intuition does not | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways]] | Replacing a failed check with a primitive substrate works only where a ground truth exists; otherwise the only lever is a peer with uncorrelated blind spots. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] | Session lifetime and mount state both silently shrink what a run attempts without producing an error, and both are fixed by moving the verdict onto a durable artifact that publishes cardinality. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact]] | A failed upload or render usually indicts the transport layer, not the file — hold the artifact fixed and remove a stage before rebuilding anything | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] | Windows laptop had no working backup: ~15 GB single-copy, nine Disabled tasks, two triggerless ones, and the Mac's manifest installed as Windows'; `/MIR`→`/E` fix | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] | Four Task Scheduler gotchas: `schtasks /Change` can't add a schedule, triggerless tasks show `Ready` forever, `G:` is per-user so SYSTEM writes nothing, sleeping laptops miss fixed-time triggers | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] | Partition cross-machine repair by capability: author all file edits from the working machine, hand the broken one a paste-ready instruction page and in-file \"nothing reads this yet\" notes | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] | A received recovery report's findings held but one explanation didn't: `dev-sync` skips an enumerated list, not underscore-prefixed folders, so `_INBOX` was already a clean pushed repo | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] | Three Windows pushes mid-session each needing a merge, including a deliberate-looking 913-file skill audit cutting ~160→63; accepted because git retains deletions, intent still to be confirmed | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] | `Ready`, `status: OK`, and a backup's own records were each locally truthful while coverage failed; narrow stage predicates don't compose into end-to-end assurance | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Submodule Conversion Breaks rsync Backup: .git Directory Becomes a File\"\naliases: [.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12, ZoteroInjestion submodule rsync failure, rsync cannot overwrite directory with file, SSD-only backup failure, okCount 80, LibraryProject DEV-nonrepo misclassification]\ntags: [backup, rsync, git, submodule, macos, keystoneos, gotcha, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-12.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-12\nupdated: 2026-09-13\n---\n\n# Submodule Conversion Breaks rsync Backup: .git Directory Becomes a File\n\nWhen `LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion` was converted into a git submodule on 2026-09-11, its `.git` changed from a directory into a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every subsequent backup run failed — but only while the CRASHBOX SSD was mounted. The 2026-09-12 `/backup` run diagnosed this, renamed the stale destination directory rather than deleting it, and completed with `status: OK`, 0 errors, and `okCount: 80`.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **A submodule conversion changes `.git` from a directory to a file.** The source-side change is legitimate and invisible locally; the damage occurs at the backup destination, where a directory of the same name already exists.\n- **rsync refuses the type change** rather than replacing the directory, so the failure is a hard error on every run until the destination is cleared.\n- **The failure class only appears when the SSD is mounted.** On SSD-absent runs the affected job class does not exist, so the backup reports clean and the defect stays invisible.\n- **The remedy was a rename, not a delete:** `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB), reversible and safe to remove later because GitHub holds the repo.\n- **`.errors.log` was archived then emptied** to clear the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning.\n- **An open manifest question remains:** `LibraryProject` is still classified as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`) though it is now a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way; the correction was offered and the answer is pending.\n\n## Details\n\nThe mechanism generalizes beyond this one repository. Any source-side operation that changes a path's *type* — directory to file, file to symlink, symlink to real file — creates a destination that a mirroring tool cannot reconcile without being told to remove what is there. The submodule conversion is an unusually clean example because the change is entirely a git-internal bookkeeping detail: nothing about the working tree's visible contents changed, the developer who performed the conversion had no reason to think about rsync, and the local repository continued to function normally. The consequence surfaced a day later, in a different subsystem, as a backup failure whose message points at a path nobody had deliberately touched. This is the same distance-from-cause property seen in [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]], where a git-level file-type decision manifested as corruption elsewhere.\n\nThe SSD-conditional visibility is the part that makes this failure durable rather than self-correcting. As established in [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] and [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]], the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes exist only when the drive is mounted; a run without the SSD has no opportunity to encounter this error and therefore reports clean. The defect thus appears intermittent when it is in fact deterministic, conditioned on a variable the operator does not think of as part of the test. An operator who sees one clean run and one failed run and concludes the failure is transient would be exactly wrong. The correct reading is the three-part one that article prescribes — status, job-class presence, manifest coverage — with the addition that a failure seen only under one hardware condition should be attributed to that condition before it is attributed to flakiness.\n\nThe rename-over-delete choice and the `.errors.log` handling both follow established discipline. Renaming 139 MB of stale destination data costs disk space and buys reversibility, the same trade taken in the Task 2 prune the same day and in the tarball snapshots of [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]]; the deletion is genuinely safe here because GitHub holds the canonical repo, but that was verified rather than assumed. Archiving `.errors.log` before emptying it follows [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]], though it is worth noting the archive-path collision hazard documented in [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] — a date-keyed archive name is only safe for one clear per day, and the `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` name carries the same once-daily assumption. The unresolved manifest misclassification (`DEV-nonrepo/` for what is now a real repo) is benign for backup correctness but is precisely the kind of drifted metadata that makes a future diagnosis harder, since the path name asserts something about the contents that is no longer true.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] — The 2026-09-11 conversion that caused this; the same change also left an open branch-pinning question.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — Why an SSD-conditional failure is invisible on SSD-absent runs, and the `okCount` cross-check that `80` here satisfies.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The `CAPTURE`/`STATE-SSD` job-class mechanism behind the conditional visibility.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg on which the stale `.git` directory sat.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The archive-then-clear procedure applied to `.errors.log`.\n- [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] — The date-keyed-archive hazard that both the log archive and the `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` name inherit.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The manifest whose `LibraryProject` classification is now stale.\n- [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] — The reversibility instinct behind renaming rather than deleting 139 MB.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-12.md]] — `/backup` session (23:30): first run failed, second succeeded after the fix, final `status: OK` / 0 errors / `okCount: 80`; lesson that the 9/11 submodule conversion changed `ZoteroInjestion`'s `.git` from directory to file and rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file; the failure only appears when CRASHBOX is mounted; stale `.git` renamed to `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB) rather than deleted; `.errors.log` archived then emptied; open question on `LibraryProject`'s `DEV-nonrepo/` manifest classification.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Verifying a Received Recovery Report: dev-sync Skip List Correction\"\naliases: [dev-sync does not skip underscore folders, _INBOX is its own repo, fix 2 unnecessary, recovery report error, erash11/_INBOX, skip list .claude _tools _DRIVE token-dashboard]\ntags: [verification, git, sync, workflow, backup, windows, cross-machine]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-14\n---\n\n# Verifying a Received Recovery Report: dev-sync Skip List Correction\n\nThe `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` report that arrived from the Windows laptop on 2026-09-14 contained a diagnostic error, caught by checking its claim against the actual sync configuration. The report asserted that the backup scripts in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` were in no repository because `dev-sync` skips folders beginning with an underscore. That is false: `dev-sync` skips a specific enumerated list — `.claude`, `_tools`, `_DRIVE`, `token-dashboard`, `*_OLD_merged*` — not an underscore *pattern*. `_INBOX` is its own repository (`github.com/erash11/_INBOX`), clean and pushed, so the report's proposed fix #2 was unnecessary.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The mistaken rule was a plausible generalization of a real list.** Several skipped entries do start with an underscore, which makes \"skips underscore folders\" a natural but wrong inference from the observed behaviour.\n- **The check was cheap and the consequence was not.** Acting on the claim would have meant relocating or re-adding files that were already tracked and pushed — and relocating them would additionally have broken the scheduled task that points at that path.\n- **Two of three root causes survived verification;** only fix #2 was dropped. A report being wrong in one place is not grounds for discarding it.\n- **The general form: a report's *findings* and its *explanations* have different reliability.** \"These scripts are unprotected\" is an observation; \"because dev-sync skips underscores\" is a theory, and the theory is where the error was.\n- **The same session's follow-up carries the same instruction** — confirm the 913-file skill-deletion commit was intentional rather than assuming either way.\n\n## Details\n\nAn enumerated exclusion list is unusually prone to this misreading because the list's members often share an incidental property. Three of the five `dev-sync` exclusions begin with an underscore, so an observer inferring the rule from behaviour — or writing a report from memory — lands on the pattern rather than the list. The failure mode is asymmetric in an instructive way: believing the pattern *over-predicts* exclusion, so it produces false alarms about unprotected files rather than false confidence about protected ones. That is the safer direction, but it still costs a wasted remediation, and in this case the remediation would have been actively harmful because moving the scripts out of `DEV\\_INBOX\\` breaks the registered scheduled task. This is close kin to [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]], where a pattern's exact matching semantics differed from the intuition about what it covered — the difference being that there the pattern silently under-matched, and here a remembered pattern over-matched.\n\nThe broader discipline is to treat an incoming report as a work queue rather than a finding list, verifying each item before acting on it — the posture already established by [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]], where four \"not a repo\" flags and five \"missing\" repos all evaporated on inspection, and by [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]], where 36 flagged conflicts collapsed to 4. What this session adds is that the same posture applies to a *prose report authored by another agent session*, not only to tool output. The report was substantially correct — its three root causes for the backup failure held up, and its inventory of what is and is not at risk was actionable — which is precisely why a blanket distrust would have been the wrong response. The productive stance is to separate what the report *observed* from what it *concluded*, and to spend verification effort on the conclusions, since those are the parts carrying the author's inference.\n\nThe session's open action item follows the same rule at a different stakes level. A commit titled \"apply global skill audit,\" authored by the user from the other machine, deleted 913 files and cut the skills pool from roughly 160 to 63. It was applied and pushed on the Mac because it appears deliberate and git retains every deleted file regardless, but the item recorded is to *confirm* intent rather than to assume it — the same \"another session may have done this on purpose, go look\" move recorded in [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]].\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — The report this correction applies to; its other findings held.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The established precedent that `dev-sync` output must be verified before acting, particularly before creative actions like `gh repo create`.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — A flagged list collapsing 36 → 4 under per-item inspection.\n- [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] — A neighbouring case where a pattern's real matching semantics diverged from the assumed rule.\n- [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] — The \"another session may have done this\" check, applied here to a 913-file deletion commit.\n- [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] — The skills-pool work whose inventory the global skill audit further reduced.\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] — A filename prefix is not the rule that governs it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): the recovery report's claim that `DEV\\_INBOX\\` scripts were in no repo because `dev-sync` skips underscore folders corrected — `dev-sync` skips only `.claude`, `_tools`, `_DRIVE`, `token-dashboard`, `*_OLD_merged*`, and `_INBOX` is its own repo (`github.com/erash11/_INBOX`), clean and pushed, making fix #2 unnecessary; a commit \"apply global skill audit\" (12:12, authored by the user from the Windows machine) deleting 913 files and cutting skills from ~160 to 63, applied and pushed on the Mac with intent to be confirmed and git retaining every deleted file.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\"\naliases: [49 unmanifested DEV folders, backup-manifest.json 78 to 125, job count 28 to 76, manifest coverage gap, AI_Analyst token-dashboard removed, DexaProject Google Drive warning, migration left folders unmanifested]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, keystoneos, verification, maintenance, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\n\nOn 2026-09-09 a KeystoneOS daily backup run returned `status: ERROR` with two problems: the `~/.claude` repo was one commit behind GitHub (blocking its push) and 49 folders under `~/DEV` were absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Rather than triage the 49, the user confirmed that all of them mattered — the gap was a residue of an earlier migration — and every one was added. The manifest grew from 78 to 125 entries and the backup's job count went from 28 to 76.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gap was invisible.** Folders absent from the manifest are never backed up, and their absence produces no error that reads as urgent — only an advisory `manifest-unmanaged` class. The 49-folder hole had existed since a prior migration.\n- **All 49 were added, not triaged:** 47 as git repos (working-tree capture to the CRASHBOX SSD) and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs` — SSD copy only, no repo semantics).\n- **Two stale entries were removed** (`AI_Analyst`, `token-dashboard`) after confirming both had already moved into `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, leaving 125 entries. Manifest hygiene runs in both directions: adding new folders and retiring moved ones.\n- **`DexaProject`'s warning was kept deliberately** — it lives on Google Drive intentionally, so its manifest complaint is an accepted permanent condition rather than a defect to silence. A separate later finding in the same day established that the *GitHub* copy of `DexaProject` still holds a patient CSV and must be deleted.\n- **The `~/.claude` behind-by-one state was resolved with `git pull --rebase --autostash`,** preserving two uncommitted edits (`settings.json`, `hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh`). Backups never auto-merge by design.\n- **Commits were made but not pushed** — `~/.claude` at `dc54c97` and `~/.claude/global-memory` at `38e462b` — with the push deliberately left to the next scheduled backup run.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the largest instance yet of the manifest-drift failure class first catalogued in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] (three folders) and re-observed at [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] (five folders). The escalation from three to five to forty-nine is informative: the earlier gaps were ordinary drift from folders created faster than manifest entries, whereas this one had a single structural cause — a migration that moved or created many directories at once while the manifest, being hand-maintained, stayed where it was. Bulk moves are precisely the event a curated list cannot survive, which strengthens the standing proposal in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] to derive the manifest from a directory scan rather than maintaining it by hand. The default-deny guard did its job in that the folders were reported rather than silently excluded, but reporting alone is only sufficient when the report is acted on, and forty-nine entries accumulating across weeks shows the report being observed and deferred.\n\nThe removal of `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` is the neglected half of manifest maintenance. A manifest entry pointing at a folder that has moved into `_ARCHIVE` will either fail loudly (noise) or resolve to a stale path (worse), and neither outcome is caught by any check that only asks \"is every folder covered?\" The `DexaProject` decision demonstrates the third state a manifest entry can occupy: an accepted, documented exception whose warning is expected output rather than a pending task. Recording the reason — it lives on Google Drive intentionally — is what prevents a future maintenance pass from either \"fixing\" it or being confused by it, the same discipline applied to the two large graph files kept deliberately in the 2026-09-01 classification pass. Notably, the same day's repo-visibility audit found that this deliberate local exception coexists with a genuine problem at a different layer: `erash11/DexaProject` is still a GitHub repository containing a patient CSV, awaiting deletion. A documented exception on one axis does not clear the artifact on every axis.\n\nThe `git pull --rebase --autostash` choice is worth isolating as policy. The backup job pushes several repos, and a repo that is behind its remote cannot push; the tempting resolutions are a force-push (destroys remote history) or a merge (creates a commit the operator did not author). Rebasing with autostash preserves both the remote's history and the two uncommitted working-tree edits, and the stated principle — backups never auto-merge — keeps the backup system from making content decisions on the operator's behalf. This is the same restraint shown when a two-writer SecondBrain conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating it, per [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]. The decision to commit `~/.claude` and `global-memory` without pushing follows the same logic in the other direction: the push is the backup's job, so leaving it to the next scheduled run keeps one owner for that action rather than racing it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the 49 folders; this run is the strongest argument yet for its auto-derivation follow-up.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original three-folder instance of the same failure class.\n- [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] — The five-folder `manifest-unmanaged` instance between that one and this.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The completeness metric this session reset from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count completeness tell, which this session extends and complicates by moving the baseline.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg that now receives working-tree captures for 47 additional repos.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same day's finding that `DexaProject` is also a live public GitHub repo holding a patient CSV.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The precedent for resolving repo divergence without adjudicating content on the operator's behalf.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: a clean run over a work set that was never complete.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): initial run `status: ERROR` with `~/.claude` one commit behind and 49 unmanifested `~/DEV` folders; user confirmed all 49 mattered (\"we did a migration earlier\"); 47 added as git repos and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs`); job count 28 → 76 and manifest 78 → 125 entries; `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` removed after confirming both had moved to `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`; `DexaProject` warning kept as an intentional Google Drive exception; `~/.claude` fixed with `git pull --rebase --autostash` preserving two uncommitted edits; commits `dc54c97` and `38e462b` left unpushed for the next backup run.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\"\naliases: [RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md, Windows no working backup, ~15 GB single copy, nine disabled KeystoneOS tasks, Mac manifest on Windows, FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md, backup_dev.ps1 MIR to E]\ntags: [backup, windows, macos, automation, keystoneos, scheduling, data-integrity, migration]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-14\n---\n\n# Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\n\nOn 2026-09-14 a `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` commit arriving from the Windows laptop revealed that the machine had **no working backup at all**. Roughly 15 GB exists in a single copy only — `SportScienceResearchRepo` (4.37 GB), `Football` (2.94 GB), `Risk_Stratification_Engine` (1.80 GB), `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` (1.69 GB), and the `~/.codex` history. Everything else — all 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Google Drive documents — is safe. The failure had three independent root causes, all of them artifacts of the Mac migration.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Nine KeystoneOS Windows scheduled tasks are Disabled** — switched off during the move to the Mac and never restored. They report their disabled state accurately; nothing ever escalated it.\n- **Two replacement tasks were registered with no trigger.** A triggerless task shows `Ready` indefinitely and will never fire — the healthiest-looking possible state for a job that does nothing.\n- **The Windows backup manifest is the Mac's manifest.** It carries `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths, with its last state written 2026-08-14 at status ERROR. `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` had likewise been overwritten by the Mac version (rsync + Mac paths) on 2026-08-05; the original is frozen at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n- **`backup_dev.ps1` was changed from `/MIR` to `/E`.** Mirroring propagates accidental deletions into the backup, which converts the backup into a second copy of a mistake rather than a recovery path. (`backup_ai_sessions.ps1` was already `/E`.)\n- **Two deliberate non-actions:** the backup scripts stay in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` because the registered scheduled task points there, and the nine disabled tasks are *not* re-enabled because the script they invoke is now the Mac's.\n\n## Details\n\nWhat makes this a distinct failure rather than another manifest-drift instance is that every layer reported truthfully and none of them reported the operational fact. A disabled task is disabled; a triggerless task is genuinely ready; a manifest containing Mac paths is a syntactically valid manifest. The only signal that would have surfaced the problem is the one nobody was checking: whether bytes actually landed in Google Drive. This is the same scope mismatch catalogued in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]], where `launchctl list` reports only the last *scheduled* run — the field answers a narrower predicate than the operator's question and is therefore silent about the gap. The recovery report's own conclusion states the rule directly: verification must confirm bytes at the destination, not that the task looks healthy.\n\nThe three causes also share a single origin. Tasks disabled \"during the Mac move,\" a manifest overwritten with Mac paths on 08-14, a `daily-backup.ps1` overwritten on 08-05 — the migration edited the Windows machine's automation surface as a side effect of syncing shared files, and nothing on the Windows side was left running to notice. This extends the pattern in [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] in the opposite direction: that article covers Windows assumptions surviving onto the Mac, whereas here Mac-shaped configuration flowed back onto Windows through the same shared repositories. Bidirectional sync between two machines with platform-specific automation means any file that encodes host facts — paths, mount points, shell dialects — is a collision waiting to happen, which is why the new `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.windows.json` was created as a *separate* file rather than a re-edit of the shared one.\n\nThe `/MIR` → `/E` change deserves isolation as policy. Robocopy's `/MIR` makes the destination an exact mirror of the source, which means a local deletion is faithfully reproduced in the backup at the next run — the backup is then a copy of the current state rather than a record of history. `/E` copies without deleting, accepting stale files in exchange for never propagating a loss. The trade-off resolves the same way as the append-only forecast log in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]: when the artifact's value is that it holds something the source might no longer have, the correct verb is never \"make identical.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] — The four scheduling mechanics that make a Windows backup task silently ineffective, and the paste-ready fix page.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — `Ready`, `Disabled`, and a valid-but-wrong manifest all report accurately on a narrower predicate than \"is the data backed up?\"\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — The mirror-image case: platform-specific configuration surviving a migration, here flowing Mac → Windows.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The Mac-side manifest whose paths ended up on the Windows machine.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The companion lesson that a backup's own status field cannot establish coverage.\n- [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] — A triggerless task and a SYSTEM-context missing `G:` both shrink the work set without producing an error.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The Mac-side SSD leg; its `/backup` run was still outstanding at session close.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` arriving from the Windows laptop; ~15 GB in single copy only (SportScienceResearchRepo 4.37 GB, Football 2.94 GB, Risk_Stratification_Engine 1.80 GB, ForcePlate_DecisionSystem 1.69 GB, `~/.codex` history) with 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Drive docs safe; three root causes (nine Disabled KeystoneOS tasks, two triggerless replacement tasks showing `Ready`, Windows manifest actually the Mac's with `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths at status ERROR 2026-08-14); `backup_dev.ps1` changed `/MIR` → `/E`; `backup-manifest.windows.json` created as documentation only; scripts kept in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` and the nine tasks left disabled because `daily-backup.ps1` was overwritten by the Mac version on 2026-08-05 (original at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\"\naliases: [CRASHBOX, SSD backup baseline, AssistantBackup, backup manifest path, volume rename mid-backup, rsync incremental resume, diskutil rename, local-only backup, Drive upload deferral]\ntags: [backup, macos, storage, automation, rsync, workflow, machine-restore]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\n\nOn 2026-08-11, as part of a machine-restore checklist closeout, an external SSD was renamed to `CRASHBOX` and used to capture the first full local backup baseline at `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`. The rename occurred while a backup run was already in flight; the run was restarted against the new path and completed clean (OK, 0 errors) at 21:25. The session also established which parts of the backup pipeline require network connectivity and which do not.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **First full CRASHBOX baseline completed clean:** OK with 0 errors at 21:25, after the backup manifest was repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup` and the in-flight run restarted.\n- **Mid-backup volume rename is safe.** Already-copied data survives the rename, and rsync's incremental behavior means a restarted run — or one interrupted by power loss — resumes rather than redoing completed work.\n- **Volume names containing spaces work fine in the PowerShell backup manifest;** the rename to `CRASHBOX` was cosmetic, not a correctness fix.\n- **Network dependency is confined to one step.** The SSD copy is fully local; Google Drive writes cache locally and upload later; only the git commit/push step needs the network, and it self-heals on the next nightly run. The machine could therefore be safely disconnected and shut down mid-pipeline.\n- **The rename was performed via a one-off manual-mode approval** rather than a standing `Bash(diskutil rename:*)` allow rule, since no recurrence was expected.\n\n## Details\n\nThe backup pipeline has three write targets with different durability and connectivity characteristics, and the session made this stratification explicit. The local SSD copy is synchronous and offline-capable. The Google Drive leg writes to a locally-cached CloudStorage path and defers the actual upload, so it neither blocks nor requires connectivity at run time — the same local-cache-then-upload behavior that previously caused a receipt read-back race when a job read a freshly-written receipt before Drive had flushed it. Only the git commit and push step genuinely needs the network, and because the backup job runs nightly, a failed push is self-healing: the next run retries it. This meant the correct answer to \"do I need internet before shutting down?\" was no, with a single deferred and self-recovering exception.\n\nThe mid-run rename was the session's operationally interesting move. Renaming a mounted volume changes the `/Volumes/<name>` mount path, which invalidates any manifest entry or in-progress run referencing the old path — but it does not touch the copied files themselves. Because the backup uses rsync semantics, restarting against the corrected path performs an incremental pass over the already-transferred data rather than a full recopy, so the cost of the restart is proportional to what remained rather than to the total. This same property makes the run resilient to power interruption, which matters for a first full baseline that transfers the largest volume of data it ever will. The rename itself was cosmetic; the manifest tolerated the prior name, spaces included.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/launchd-backup-receipt-race-repo-reconciliation]] — The same backup subsystem, where a Google Drive CloudStorage write-lag raced a receipt read-back and the job was moved under `launchd`; this article records the local-SSD leg and its offline-capable characteristics.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The rename was gated by the permission system and resolved via a one-off manual-mode approval rather than a standing allow rule.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The broader scheduled-automation stack (nightly backup task, reindex, cloud checks) that this baseline run belongs to.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why durable, long-running backup work belongs in an OS-scheduled job rather than an in-session background command.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Restore-closeout session (18:04): SSD renamed to `CRASHBOX`, manifest repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`, in-flight run restarted, network-dependency analysis (local SSD / deferred Drive upload / self-healing git push), first full baseline completed OK with 0 errors at 21:25.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"status: OK Is Not Proof of a Full Backup — Read okCount\"\naliases: [okCount cross-check, status OK insufficient, backup job count baseline drift, error count cannot detect missing SSD, 9 vs 31 vs 76 jobs, two independent completeness axes]\ntags: [backup, automation, verification, macos, monitoring, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# status: OK Is Not Proof of a Full Backup — Read okCount\n\nThe KeystoneOS daily backup reports a `status` field and an `okCount`, and the 2026-09-09 session restated as explicit policy that the former is insufficient on its own. A run can report `status: OK` with zero errors while having captured only a fraction of the intended data, because the error count measures the outcome of attempted jobs and cannot detect a job that was never attempted. The cross-check is `okCount`: roughly 31 jobs meant the CRASHBOX SSD was captured, roughly 9–10 meant it was absent and only repo pushes plus Google Drive snapshots ran.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The error count is structurally blind to a missing target.** An unmounted SSD removes whole job classes from the run; every remaining job succeeds, so the aggregate verdict is clean and wrong.\n- **`okCount` is the completeness signal, `status` is the execution signal.** They answer different questions and both must be read.\n- **The expected job count is not a constant.** The same session that codified the tell moved the full-run baseline from 28 to 76 jobs by adding 49 manifest entries, so any remembered number expires.\n- **There are two independent completeness axes:** whether the SSD was mounted (job classes present or absent) and whether the manifest is complete (folders in scope or not). A run can be full on one axis and empty on the other.\n\n## Details\n\nThis lesson has now been learned twice from different directions, and the pairing is what makes it durable. The 2026-09-04 finding recorded in [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] arrived at the job count from the hardware side: a charge-only USB-C cable left the CRASHBOX SSD unmounted, the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes silently did not exist, and a clean run saved nothing that was uncommitted, non-repo, or gitignored. The 2026-09-09 finding arrives from the manifest side: the SSD was fine, but 49 folders were never in the instruction set, so their absence was equally invisible to the error count. In both cases the failure is that the denominator shrank rather than the numerator falling, which no ratio-free success indicator can express — the same defect described generally in [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]].\n\nThe baseline drift is the practical complication. On 2026-09-07 the tell was hardened by writing the *mechanism* into `SKILL.md` — the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` classes exist only when the drive is mounted — precisely so the check would not depend on remembering that 31 is good. Two days later the manifest expansion vindicated that decision by moving a full run to 76 jobs, which would have invalidated a memorized threshold overnight. The generalizable rule, stated in [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]], is that a quantity used as a health check should be recorded at whatever abstraction level is stable: \"the SSD job classes are missing\" survives a manifest change, \"expect 31\" does not. The correct reading of a backup run is therefore three-part — did the attempted jobs succeed (`status`), were the SSD-dependent job classes present (`okCount` shape), and does the manifest still cover every folder that matters (`manifest-unmanaged` entries) — with no single field standing in for the other two.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The origin of the job-count tell and its `CAPTURE`/`STATE-SSD` mechanism, discovered from the unmounted-drive side.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The manifest-side instance from the same session, which also reset the expected job count from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — A sibling case where a different backup status field answered a narrower question than the operator's.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general pattern: an accurate field reporting on a narrower predicate than the one being asked.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — Why a clean verdict over a shrunken work set is the recurring shape here.\n- [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]] — Why the tell was written as a mechanism rather than a threshold, validated by the baseline change.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): explicit lesson that `status: OK` alone is not proof of a full backup and `okCount` must be cross-checked (~31 jobs = SSD captured, ~9–10 = SSD absent with only repo pushes and Drive snapshots), that the error count cannot detect a missing SSD, and that manifest coverage is a parallel silent failure mode; job count moved 28 → 76 in the same session.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Harness-Agnostic AIOS Plan (Mac + Windows Skills/Backup Stack)\"\naliases: [harness-agnostic-aios, 2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md, AIOS plan, skills repo cross-platform plan, 42 own skills 19 third-party, Task 2 prune 306 to 208, claudex-loop codex-review out of scope]\ntags: [keystoneos, claude-code, skills, cross-platform, planning, git, macos, windows]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-12.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-12\nupdated: 2026-09-13\n---\n\n# Harness-Agnostic AIOS Plan (Mac + Windows Skills/Backup Stack)\n\nThe \"harness-agnostic AIOS\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) is a multi-task effort to make the skills and backup stack function identically on both the Mac and the Windows machine. Tasks 1–2 were completed on 2026-09-12; the plan itself remained unapproved at round 10 of its review gate, having accumulated a scope error, a backup-breaking safety hook, and several smaller defects that review surfaced before implementation.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The ownership inventory was wrong until review corrected it:** of 19 outward symlinks in the skills repo, 14 are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). The corrected framing is **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links**.\n- **Task 4's inversion script was deleted entirely** rather than fixed — copying third-party vendored links into a personal repo was the wrong operation, not a buggy one. The four vendored links (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay out of `claude-skills` under the same rule that excludes plugins; Windows gets a separate install of the providing package.\n- **Task 2's prune took the global skills pool 306 → 208**, with 61 skills still resolving and roughly 150 third-party installs untouched. It was executed as a **move, not a delete**, keeping the operation reversible.\n- **Round-10 findings, still open:** a serious one (the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup — see below), plus wording that still said `.gitignore` where `.git/info/exclude` is correct, and a Pi test user likely inheriting the `staff` group and stalling a boundary step.\n- **The dead symlinks were already committed and pushed** before this session; the decision converts a silent Windows breakage into an explicitly documented one with a stated remedy.\n\n## Details\n\nThe plan's central defect was a scope error rather than a logic error, and it is the kind that only surfaces when someone asks who owns each file. A skills repository that mixes hand-authored skills with package-manager-installed ones presents a uniform surface — every entry is a symlink, and every symlink looks equally like a candidate for a \"make this work on Windows too\" transformation. Task 4 would therefore have copied 14 third-party installs into a personal repository, mixing vendor-managed content into a tree whose whole value is that its contents are authored and maintained locally. The corrected inventory (42 own, 19 third-party) is the durable artifact here, and it should be re-derived rather than remembered, since the vendored count moves whenever a package is installed or dropped — the same reasoning that made the backup job count unsafe to memorize in [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]].\n\nThe decision to exclude the four vendored links extends an existing rule rather than inventing one. Plugins are already excluded from the skills repo because they are installed, not authored; the vendored symlinks are the same category wearing a different shape. The consequence — those four skills are dead links on Windows until the providing package is installed there — is accepted deliberately, which matters because the links had already been committed and pushed and were *already* dead on Windows. The change is from an undocumented breakage to a documented one with a named remedy, which is the same disposition applied to `DexaProject`'s permanent manifest warning in [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]]: an accepted condition recorded with its reason is not a defect.\n\nTask 2's prune demonstrates the reversibility discipline that recurs throughout this stack. Moving 98 entries out of the global pool rather than deleting them means an incorrect prune costs a move back rather than a restore from backup, and it sits alongside the tarball snapshots of [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] as the same instinct applied at a lower stakes level. The residual 61-resolving / ~150-untouched split is also the number to verify after any future prune, since it is the ratio rather than the absolute that describes a healthy pool.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] — The exclusion mechanism this plan depends on, and the trailing-slash defect that would have silently re-added the dead links.\n- [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] — The restore test run against this plan's backup, which exposed a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n- [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] — The review gate that caught the Task 4 scope error after a provider switch.\n- [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — The `~/.agents/skills` → Claude → Codex symlink topology whose vendored half this plan excludes.\n- [[concepts/skills-repo-git-hygiene]] — Prior hygiene work in the same repo, including the explicit-path staging discipline that keeps unowned content out of commits.\n- [[concepts/skills-repo-worktree-branch-cleanup]] — The preceding cleanup pass on the same repo and its `dcg` permission gate.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The `mv`-with-variable-paths block hit during Task 2, worked around with Python containment checks.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-12.md]] — Memory maintenance session (17:31): plan `00730b6` pushed, Tasks 1–2 complete, reviewer's discovery that 14 of 19 outward symlinks are third-party, inventory reframed as 42 own / 19 third-party, Task 4 inversion script deleted, four vendored links excluded with a separate Windows install as the remedy, Task 2 prune executed as a move (306 → 208 global pool, 61 resolving, ~150 third-party untouched), and three round-10 findings left open.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Environment Maintenance\"\naliases: [doctor diagnostics, plugin cache migration, mcp re-auth, effort setting, github MCP plugin, GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN, gh CLI vs MCP, fable-5 model switch, advisor incompatibility, orphan temp_github folders, github MCP 401 header, OAuth fallback disabled]\ntags: [claude-code, tooling, maintenance, infrastructure]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-03.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-09.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-07-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# Claude Code Environment Maintenance\n\nPeriodic maintenance of the Claude Code environment surfaces configuration-drift issues that accumulate silently between active sessions. Four notable maintenance passes have been documented: a 2026-06-10 session that resolved plugin cache migration, stale MCP authentications, and temp-directory accumulation; a 2026-06-03 session that diagnosed and resolved a missing `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` for the GitHub MCP plugin; a 2026-06-09 session that switched the default model to Fable 5 and discovered that the advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with Fable 5 requests; and a 2026-07-09 observation of the GitHub MCP plugin failing to reconnect with an HTTP 401 because a configured `Authorization` header disables OAuth fallback. Most issues are caught via `/doctor` and resolved through `/mcp`, `/plugins`, or targeted configuration changes.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- `/doctor` flagged 4 issues on 2026-06-10: three claude.ai MCP servers (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) needing re-authentication, and the `cli-printing-press` plugin not found at its expected versioned cache path.\n- Claude Code migrated its plugin cache from a flat layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\`) to a versioned layout (`cache\\<plugin>\\<plugin>\\<version>\\`), likely due to a Claude Code update. Plugins installed under the old layout fail version checks even though their skills may still work via a fallback path; the fix is to reinstall via `/plugins`.\n- Four stale `temp_github_*` directories were found in the plugins cache, left over from interrupted installs in late May/early June — safe to delete once confirmed unused. Interrupted plugin downloads can both lose a plugin's cache entry and leave orphan `temp_github_*` folders; `/plugin` reinstall + `/reload-plugins` restores the cache, and the orphan folders require manual deletion.\n- For MCP servers not actively used from Claude Code (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar), disconnecting them in claude.ai's connector settings permanently silences `/doctor` re-auth warnings and eliminates the tool-schema token overhead of loading unused MCP tool definitions each session.\n- The `compound-engineering` plugin was installed via `/plugin` and activated with `/reload-plugins`, adding `ce-*` skills/agents for planning, code review, commits, and PR workflows. `/effort` was set to \"high\" as the new session default.\n- **GitHub MCP plugin vs. `gh` CLI (2026-06-03):** the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP plugin requires a `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable to pass `/doctor`. If all GitHub operations use the `gh` CLI directly (authenticated via `gh auth login`; scopes: gist, read:org, repo, workflow), the MCP plugin is redundant — the cleanest fix is disabling it with `\"github@claude-plugins-official\": false` in `.claude/settings.json`. Alternatively, set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` from an existing PAT (e.g., reuse `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` if it has matching scopes).\n- **Fable 5 + medium effort + advisor incompatibility (2026-06-09):** default model switched to Fable 5, effort set to medium. The advisor model (opus-4-8) is incompatible with the Fable 5 request model — requests return 400 errors — and was disabled. When switching request models, the advisor model must also be verified for compatibility or disabled; mismatched advisor+request combinations produce API errors that are not immediately obvious as a configuration conflict.\n- **GitHub MCP 401 with Authorization header disables OAuth (2026-07-09):** the `plugin:github:github` MCP server failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 — the server rejected the configured `Authorization` header, and OAuth fallback is disabled whenever `headers.Authorization` is explicitly set. Fix: check/refresh the token, or remove the `headers.Authorization` config entirely to re-enable OAuth negotiation.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `/doctor` command acts as a quick health check across MCP connections, plugin installs, and cache state. On 2026-06-10 it caught a cache-format mismatch that was not the user's fault: a Claude Code update changed how plugins are cached on disk (from a flat directory per plugin to a versioned subdirectory), and `cli-printing-press` had been installed before that change. The plugin's files were technically present, but the version-check logic looked in the new versioned path and didn't find them — the recommended fix was simply to reinstall the plugin via `/plugins` so it lands in the new layout. The MCP re-auth findings (Supabase, Vercel, Google Calendar) were treated as low priority: rather than re-authenticating connectors that may not be exercised from Claude Code at all, the suggested path was to disconnect them at the source (claude.ai connector settings), which both stops the recurring `/doctor` warning and reduces the standing tool-schema footprint in every session.\n\nOn 2026-06-03, a separate `/doctor` run flagged the `github@claude-plugins-official` MCP server as invalid — specifically, no `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` environment variable was set. Investigation confirmed that all GitHub interactions in this environment already flow through the `gh` CLI (authenticated separately in `~/.config/gh/`), making the GitHub MCP plugin effectively unused. The two resolution options are: (1) disable the plugin entirely in `.claude/settings.json` — the simplest fix when the plugin genuinely adds no value; or (2) set `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` using an existing PAT with compatible scopes, avoiding a new token generation. In this case, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` already existed with sufficient scopes, so `setx GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN <value>` was used. Note: `setx` writes to the Windows registry immediately but requires fully restarting VS Code for the change to propagate to the Claude Code process — see [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] for the stale-environment gotcha this can trigger.\n\nOn 2026-06-09, a maintenance session switched the default model from Sonnet-tier to Fable 5 and changed effort to medium. The advisor feature, which provides secondary-model review of responses, uses a separately-specified model (opus-4-8 in this configuration). When the request model is Fable 5, the opus-4-8 advisor returns 400 API errors because the two models are incompatible in the advisor/request pairing. The advisor was disabled as a result. The lesson: model switches require verifying the full model configuration stack — not just the primary request model but also any secondary models (advisor, sub-agent defaults, tool-use overrides) that may have incompatible pairings.\n\nOn 2026-07-09, the GitHub MCP plugin surfaced a distinct failure mode from the 2026-06-03 missing-token case: here a token/header *was* configured, but the plugin failed to reconnect with HTTP 401 because the server rejected the supplied `Authorization` header. The non-obvious mechanism is that setting `headers.Authorization` explicitly disables the plugin's OAuth fallback — the plugin assumes a manually-supplied header means the user wants header-based auth exclusively, so it does not fall back to OAuth negotiation when the header is rejected. The result is a hard 401 with no automatic recovery. The two fixes are to refresh the token behind the header, or to remove the `headers.Authorization` config so OAuth negotiation is re-enabled. This reinforces the broader 2026-06-03 finding that the GitHub MCP plugin is often redundant when `gh` CLI already handles all GitHub operations — disabling the plugin outright sidesteps both the missing-token and the header-401 failure modes.\n\nTogether these maintenance passes confirm the same operational pattern: the `/doctor` command's value is proportional to how regularly it is run and how promptly its findings are triaged. Issues like the plugin cache migration, the unused GitHub MCP plugin, the advisor incompatibility, the header-401 reconnect failure, and the orphan temp folders can accumulate silently for weeks without causing visible failures, but each unresolved warning represents either a real reliability risk or unnecessary token overhead that degrades session quality.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] — Skill creation later on the same 2026-06-10 maintenance day depended on plugins and skills registering correctly, which this maintenance pass helped ensure.\n- [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] — The on-ramp automation pilot run later on 2026-06-10 operates inside this same Claude Code harness.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — An earlier (2026-04-14) setup of the persistent-memory hook system that lives in the same `~/.claude/` configuration this maintenance pass audited.\n- [[concepts/windows-setx-env-propagation]] — The Windows registry env-var propagation gotcha encountered while applying the `GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` fix on 2026-06-03.\n- [[concepts/book-advisor-system]] — The `/advisor` invocation on 2026-07-09 that surfaced the GitHub MCP 401 also confirmed the current advisor library state (one persona).\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] — `/doctor` diagnostics, plugin cache fix, MCP re-auth guidance, `compound-engineering` plugin install, `/effort` default change.\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Source of the earlier memory-compiler hook setup referenced above.\n- [[daily/2026-06-03.md]] — GitHub MCP plugin `/doctor` warning, `gh` CLI vs. MCP investigation, `CODEX_GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN` reuse via `setx`, and stale VS Code environment diagnosis (~15:00 session).\n- [[daily/2026-06-09.md]] — Fable 5 model switch (14:07): effort set to medium, opus-4-8 advisor disabled due to 400 API incompatibility with Fable 5, orphan `temp_github_*` folders confirmed and deleted.\n- [[daily/2026-07-09.md]] — GitHub MCP 401 reconnect failure (09:35): server rejects configured `Authorization` header, OAuth fallback disabled when `headers.Authorization` is set; fix is token refresh or header removal.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"The /backup Skill: Job Count as the Completeness Signal\"\naliases: [/backup skill, CRASHBOX not mounted, 9 jobs vs 31 jobs, CAPTURE and STATE-SSD job classes, diskutil list external empty, charge-only USB-C cable, run-result file not exit code, errors.log archived-2026-09-04, skill loads immediately no restart]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, skills, hardware, verification, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-04.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-07.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-04\nupdated: 2026-09-07\n---\n\n# The /backup Skill: Job Count as the Completeness Signal\n\nOn 2026-09-04 at 16:17, a partially-failed scheduled backup was re-run manually and the experience was turned into a reusable `/backup` skill. The session's central finding is a measurement lesson: a backup run reporting zero errors can still be silently incomplete when a target volume is unmounted, so the number of jobs completed — 9 without the CRASHBOX SSD attached versus 31 with it — is a better health signal than the error count. A secondary thread diagnosed the SSD's non-appearance as a charge-only USB-C cable rather than any software fault. On 2026-09-07 the tell was written into the skill file itself, with the mechanism identified and verified against real logs.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The scheduled job was fine; the network was not.** `com.keystone.dailybackup` fired on time at 09:05. Two repo pushes (`global-memory`, `skills`) failed from a transient network/DNS blip, and a manual re-run cleared them — the `.errors.log` entry described a symptom, not a broken scheduler.\n- **Zero errors ≠ complete.** With CRASHBOX unmounted, committed work still reached GitHub and Google Drive, but uncommitted, non-repo, and gitignored files were saved *nowhere*. The run reported no errors because every job it knew to attempt succeeded.\n- **Job count is the completeness metric:** 9 jobs OK without the SSD, 31 jobs OK with it. The gap is the entire local-only-file leg of the backup.\n- **The mechanism behind the tell (recorded 2026-09-07):** the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes only exist when the drive is mounted, so their absence is what shrinks the count. Verified against two real runs — a 13:25 run without the SSD produced 4 repo pushes plus 6 Drive snaps (10 jobs), and a 13:46 run with it produced the same 10 plus 20 `CAPTURE` and 9 `STATE-SSD` jobs. This tell is now documented in `~/.claude/skills/backup/SKILL.md` rather than living only in the operator's head.\n- **The skill checks the run-result file, not exit codes,** reports skipped targets explicitly, and clears the stale `.errors.log` entry so session-start stops nagging about a resolved condition.\n- **A USB-C drive that powers on but never mounts is almost always a charge-only cable.** `diskutil list external` printing nothing at all — no external disk visible whatsoever — is the fast discriminator between a cable/port problem and a filesystem problem. Swapping the cable fixed it.\n- **A newly written skill file loads immediately** — no session restart is needed before invoking it.\n- Naming followed the nearest sibling: lowercase-kebab `backup` to match `claude-stack-doctor`, since no `_ALLCAPS` private skills exist in this workspace.\n\n## Details\n\nThe unmounted-target failure is the most transferable finding, and it is a specific instance of a status field answering a narrower question than the operator's. \"Did the backup succeed?\" was answered as \"did every attempted job succeed?\", which is true and useless when the set of attempted jobs is silently smaller than the set of required jobs. The nine-versus-thirty-one gap is not a partial degradation of coverage — it is precisely the class of data that has no other copy anywhere. Committed repository content is redundantly protected by GitHub and Google Drive, so the SSD leg exists specifically to capture uncommitted work, non-repo files, and gitignored artifacts. Losing exactly that leg while reporting a clean run is the worst possible arrangement of signal and reality, and it is why the new skill reports skipped targets explicitly rather than folding them into an aggregate verdict. This is the same defect shape as the backup manifest omission documented in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]]: in both cases coverage was wrong while execution was perfect, and in both cases the aggregate success indicator was structurally incapable of noticing.\n\nThe 2026-09-07 follow-up hardened the tell from a remembered number into a documented, mechanism-backed check. Knowing that \"31 means good, 9 means bad\" is fragile — the totals shift as repos and Drive targets are added — whereas knowing that the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes are conditional on the mount makes the check robust to changes in the other job counts. Writing it into `SKILL.md` also moves the knowledge to where the next operator (or the next session) will encounter it, rather than requiring recall. The verification method matters too: rather than reasoning from the manifest about what *should* run, the claim was checked against two real log entries taken minutes apart under both conditions, which is a natural A/B that isolates exactly the SSD-dependent jobs.\n\nThe hardware diagnosis contributes a small, reusable triage rule. When an external drive shows power indicators but never mounts, the instinct is to suspect the filesystem, the mount daemon, or permissions. `diskutil list external` returning *nothing* rules all of those out at once: a drive with a corrupt filesystem still enumerates as a device, so total absence from the device list places the fault below the software layer entirely — at the cable, the port, or the hub. USB-C makes this especially common because charge-only cables are physically identical to data-capable ones. The prescribed order — swap to a known data cable first, then change port or connect directly rather than through a hub — moves from cheapest to most disruptive, and the cable swap resolved it here.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The closest prior instance: coverage failure with clean execution, and a status field scoped more narrowly than the operational question.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The first full baseline of this same SSD leg; documents the rsync-incremental behavior that makes re-running after a missed run cheap.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — Why the SSD leg is the only protection during a deferred-commit window, when `ahead=0` reads green over an uncommitted working tree.\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The archive-then-clear discipline the skill automates for `.errors.log`.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The sibling health-check skill whose naming convention `/backup` followed, and which surfaces backup freshness as a standing check.\n- [[concepts/offline-capable-pipeline-legs]] — The per-leg network-dependency framing; the transient push failure and the unmounted SSD are failures in two different legs with different remedies.\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — The general rule the skill implements: reconcile against a durable artifact rather than trusting an exit code.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The \"0 errors\" verdict answering a narrower predicate than \"is the backup complete?\".\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The shared shape across backup jobs, manifest folders, and lockfile-invisible skills.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-04.md]] — Manual backup and `/backup` skill session (16:17): `com.keystone.dailybackup` confirmed to have fired on time at 09:05 with two transient push failures cleared by a re-run, CRASHBOX unattached leaving uncommitted/non-repo/gitignored files unsaved, 9-versus-31 job count as the completeness signal, `diskutil list external` empty diagnosing a charge-only cable, lowercase-kebab naming after `claude-stack-doctor`, skill reading the run-result file and reporting skipped targets, stale `.errors.log` cleared with archive at `.errors.log.archived-2026-09-04`, immediate skill load without restart, and the final receipt `run-20260904-134638.txt` (7,801 files, 0 errors, 31 jobs OK).\n- [[daily/2026-09-07.md]] — Config-hygiene session (18:05): the job-count tell written into `~/.claude/skills/backup/SKILL.md`, mechanism identified as the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes existing only when the drive is mounted, verified against a 13:25 run without the SSD (4 repo pushes + 6 Drive snaps) versus a 13:46 run with it (the same 10 plus 20 `CAPTURE` + 9 `STATE-SSD`), a stale `SKILL.md.bak-2026-08-29` deleted from the stack-doctor skill, and a `/claude-stack-doctor` run confirming backup OK with 31 targets.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/backup-verification-by-restore.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Verify a Backup by Restoring It: the 57-of-61 Two-Hop Symlink Gap\"\naliases: [restore test 57 of 61 skills, two-hop symlink chain unbacked vendor folder, vendor-review not backed up, backup completeness by restore, restore-test discipline]\ntags: [backup, verification, symlinks, skills, macos, recovery, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-12.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-12\nupdated: 2026-09-13\n---\n\n# Verify a Backup by Restoring It: the 57-of-61 Two-Hop Symlink Gap\n\nDuring the 2026-09-12 harness-agnostic AIOS work, a skills backup was tested by actually restoring it rather than by inspecting the archive. The restore produced 57 of an expected 61 skills. The four missing entries traced to a two-hop symlink chain — `~/.claude/skills/X → ~/.agents/skills/X → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...` — whose final hop landed in a vendor folder that was not in the backup set at all.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Only the restore surfaced the gap.** The backup itself reported no error; the archive contained every path it was asked to contain. The shortfall existed one indirection beyond the backup's scope.\n- **The failure was a two-hop chain.** A single-hop check would have found the `~/.agents/skills/X` target present and concluded the entry was covered; the second hop into `~/.agents/vendor-review/` is where coverage stopped.\n- **57 of 61 is a ratio the backup could not compute,** because the four missing skills were never absent from the backup's own accounting — they were absent from the set it was told to cover.\n- **The stated rule is procedural:** backups must be verified by actually restoring them, not by trusting a clean run or an archive listing.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the strongest available demonstration of a rule the backup stack has approached from several directions: a success report describes the jobs that were attempted, never the jobs that should have existed. [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] established that `status: OK` cannot detect an unmounted drive, and [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] established that it cannot detect 49 folders missing from the manifest. The restore test closes the loop by measuring the only quantity that actually matters — how much of the thing comes back — and it does so without needing to know in advance what kind of omission to look for. A restore that returns 57 of 61 is evidence of *some* gap; the diagnosis of which gap follows afterward. That property is what makes restore testing categorically different from every check that inspects the backup's own records, all of which can only report on what they were told about.\n\nThe two-hop chain explains why an intermediate check would have been reassuring and wrong. Symlink resolution is transitive at the filesystem level but not at the auditing level: a script that verifies \"every entry in `~/.claude/skills` has a live target\" stops at `~/.agents/skills/X`, finds it, and reports coverage, because that path *is* in the backup. Only following the chain to its terminus reveals that the content lives in `~/.agents/vendor-review/`, outside the manifest. This is the same shape as the aggregate-success defect described in [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]], displaced by one level of indirection — the work set was not merely shrunken, it was shrunken at a depth the checker did not descend to.\n\nThe finding also sharpens the vendored-skill topology recorded in [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]]. That article documents the deliberate `~/.agents/skills` → `~/.claude/skills` → `~/.codex/skills` arrangement, chosen so that a single indirection point governs relocation. The cost of that design, now visible, is that backup coverage must extend to wherever the *canonical* files live, which for vendor-installed skills is a third location that the hand-install pattern does not mention. Either `vendor-review` enters the manifest or the four skills are accepted as reinstallable-from-source rather than restorable — and that choice, like the `DexaProject` exception, should be written down next to the manifest rather than left to be rediscovered by the next restore test.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] — The plan whose backup this restore test was verifying.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — Why a clean status is not evidence of completeness; the restore test is the check that does not share that blindness.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The manifest-scope failure mode the missing `vendor-review` folder instantiates.\n- [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — The symlink topology whose third hop falls outside the backup set.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general pattern, here occurring one indirection below where the checker looked.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The same distrust applied to an intermediate layer's self-report.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-12.md]] — Memory maintenance session (17:31): lesson that backups must be verified by actually restoring them; the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X → ~/.agents/skills/X → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Cross-Machine Push Collisions During a Session\"\naliases: [three pushes from Windows mid-session, merge on every push, something running unattended, apply global skill audit commit, 913 files deleted 160 to 63 skills]\ntags: [git, sync, cross-machine, session-continuity, workflow, windows, macos]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-14\n---\n\n# Cross-Machine Push Collisions During a Session\n\nThree separate pushes arrived from the Windows laptop during the 2026-09-14 Mac session, each requiring a merge on the Mac side. One of them — `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` — was the artifact that set the session's agenda; another, timestamped 12:12 and titled \"apply global skill audit,\" deleted 913 files and cut the global skills pool from roughly 160 to 63. The recorded observation is that it is worth knowing whether something is running unattended on the other machine.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Three pushes in one session is a rate, and the rate is the signal.** Occasional cross-machine pushes are expected; a steady arrival during a single sitting suggests an automated or agent-driven writer rather than a person at a keyboard.\n- **Every arrival cost a merge.** The friction is not the merge itself but that the Mac session's working state was repeatedly interrupted by content it did not author.\n- **The largest arrival was a 913-file deletion.** It was applied and pushed because it appears deliberate, and because git retains every deleted skill if recovery is needed — the destructive action is recoverable, so the safe move is to accept and verify rather than block.\n- **One of the pushes was the session's most useful input.** The same channel that caused the collisions delivered `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md`, so the answer is not to close the channel.\n- **The open question was recorded rather than resolved:** confirm the skill audit was intentional; nothing is lost either way.\n\n## Details\n\nTwo machines pushing to the same repositories is the intended topology — it is what makes the shared router files, the memory tier, and the cross-machine handoff queue work at all. The new information here is what that topology feels like when the other end is active: a session that expects to be the sole writer finds itself merging three times, and each merge is a small decision about content whose provenance and intent are not visible from this side. The \"apply global skill audit\" commit is the extreme case, since a 913-file deletion arriving unannounced is indistinguishable, at the git layer, between a deliberate audit and a misfired script. What made it safe to accept is a property of the substrate rather than of the commit: git retains deleted content, so accepting is reversible and blocking would only delay the same decision. That is the same reversibility calculus that made the 306 → 208 skills prune a *move* rather than a delete in [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]].\n\nThe unattended-activity question is worth asking because it changes how other signals should be read. If an automated process is pushing from the Windows machine, then a repo that appears behind may self-correct, a divergence error may self-clear, and an unfamiliar file may have an owner other than the last human session — all conditions previously observed and recorded in [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] and [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]]. In a single-writer world, an unexplained artifact is an anomaly to investigate; in a multi-writer world, it is first a question about which writer produced it. The practical consequence is that the \"check whether another session already handled this\" step stops being an occasional courtesy and becomes the default first move.\n\nThere is a specific tension in this particular session worth naming. The Windows machine was simultaneously discovered to have *no functioning scheduled automation* — nine disabled tasks and two triggerless ones — and to be pushing three times in an afternoon. Those facts do not contradict each other (interactive agent sessions push; scheduled backups do not), but together they sharpen the question: something over there is active, and it is not the backup.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — The same Windows machine, whose scheduled automation is entirely dead while its interactive pushes continue.\n- [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] — The verification posture applied to content arriving through this same channel.\n- [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] — The skills-pool work this 913-file audit further reduces; the reversible-move discipline it established.\n- [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] — \"Another session may have fixed it\" as a recorded action item rather than an assumption.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — Self-clearing divergence errors, a direct consequence of another writer being active.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The shared-repo topology that makes both the collisions and the handoffs possible.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — Why a sync indicator says less than it appears to when writers are plural.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): three separate pushes arriving from the Windows laptop during the session, each requiring a merge, with the note that it is worth knowing whether something is running unattended there; a commit \"apply global skill audit\" (12:12, authored by the user from the other machine) deleting 913 files and cutting skills from ~160 to 63 plus writing `project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md`, applied and pushed on the Mac, appearing deliberate with git retaining every deleted skill; action item to confirm the audit was intentional.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/skills-repo-git-hygiene.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Skills Repo Git Hygiene: Worktree-Routed Edits and Explicit-Path Staging\"\naliases: [.claude/worktrees gitignore, nested repo hazard, background-isolation guard, explicit-path staging, skills repo commits, worktree-codex-direct-routing, anthropic-skills portability, mattpocock-skills Codex exclusions]\ntags: [git, claude-code, skills, workflow, tooling, worktree]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-15\nupdated: 2026-08-15\n---\n\n# Skills Repo Git Hygiene: Worktree-Routed Edits and Explicit-Path Staging\n\nThe 2026-08-15 wrap-up of a multi-session effort on the `~/.claude/skills` git repo established several durable practices for working in a repository that is simultaneously a live configuration directory and a version-controlled artifact. Edits are routed through git worktrees because a background-isolation guard blocks direct edits to the shared checkout; the `.claude/` directory is excluded from commits by explicit-path staging and then permanently by `.gitignore`, because `.claude/worktrees/` holds four live git worktrees that would nest repositories if committed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`.claude/worktrees/` must be gitignored.** It held four live git worktrees at the time of the session; committing it would nest repositories inside the repository. The exclusion was first achieved by explicit-path staging (deliberately naming every file to stage rather than using `git add -A`), then made permanent with a `.gitignore` line in commit `163db25`.\n- **The background-isolation guard blocks direct edits to the shared checkout**, so skill edits route through a git worktree off `main` rather than being applied in place.\n- **Commits shipped:** `faf6be8` (routing + description trims), `2c5a10a` (skill-authoring consolidation), `008f345` (doctor-plus, playwright-cli, presets gallery, 14 symlinks), `163db25` (`.gitignore`). `main` ended in sync with origin, working tree clean.\n- **Pre-existing uncommitted work was committed only after diff review plus a credential scan** (private repo, clean) — four to five files that had been sitting in the working tree across sessions.\n- **Two self-caught mistakes worth recording:** recommending removal of `anthropic-skills` (which would have destroyed portability), and a trim pass that silently dropped 20 trigger phrases while claiming it had not. The second was caught by the Codex review gate rather than by self-report.\n- **Open lever on Codex exclusions:** dropping `mattpocock-skills` is the only remaining option for reducing Codex's 171 excluded skills (down from 187) — undecided.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `~/.claude/skills` repository occupies an unusual position: it is the live configuration that the running agent reads from, and it is a git repository synced daily across machines. This dual role generates the hygiene requirements. Because it is live configuration, a background-isolation guard prevents direct writes to the shared checkout — an agent editing skills in place could alter the behavior of the very session performing the edit. Routing through a worktree off `main` isolates the edit until it is deliberately merged. Because it is a synced repository, deletions have cross-machine blast radius, which is why the [[concepts/browser-automation-stack-audit-mac]] remediation chose to neuter rather than delete two non-functional skills: the Windows machine may still have their dependencies.\n\nThe `.claude/worktrees/` problem is a specific instance of a general git hazard. Worktrees contain their own git metadata; committing a directory that holds four of them would embed repository state inside the tracked tree, producing the nested-repo confusion that git handles poorly and that is difficult to unwind after the fact. The two-step remedy is instructive: explicit-path staging solved it for the immediate commits (naming every intended file rather than relying on `git add -A` or `git add .`), and the `.gitignore` line made it structural so future sessions cannot reintroduce it by accident. Explicit-path staging generalizes as a discipline for any repository where the working tree contains a mixture of tracked source and live runtime state — the same class of caution as the [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] snapshotting, applied at the staging boundary rather than the destruction boundary. Committing the four-to-five pre-existing uncommitted files only after a diff review and a credential scan reflects the same posture: a private repo is not a reason to skip the scan, only a reason for the scan to be expected to come back clean.\n\nThe two self-caught mistakes are worth preserving because they illustrate different detection paths. The `anthropic-skills` removal recommendation was caught by reasoning — removing it would have destroyed the portability that the whole Codex/Claude Code effort was built to establish, so the consequence was visible before execution. The dropped trigger phrases were caught only by an external review gate, because the trimming pass's own account of its work was wrong: it reported preserving phrases it had in fact deleted. This is the same shape as the [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] pattern — an intermediate layer's self-report is not evidence — and it is the reason the outstanding batch trim of 36 over-ceiling skills described in [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] needs diff-level verification rather than a completion claim.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/skill-description-budget-ceiling]] — The substantive work these commits carried; the outstanding 36-skill batch trim will need the same worktree routing and diff verification.\n- [[concepts/browser-automation-stack-audit-mac]] — The daily cross-machine sync of this repo is precisely why dead skills were to be neutered rather than deleted.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — Prior worktree failure modes in the same environment; the `.claude/worktrees/` gitignore addition parallels the earlier one made for the same reason.\n- [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] — Explicit-path staging is the staging-boundary analogue of snapshotting before destructive git operations.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The trim pass's false self-report about trigger phrases, caught by an independent review gate.\n- [[concepts/committed-symlink-repo-landmine]] — A prior incident in the same skills-repo lineage where what was committed (mode-120000 symlinks) caused cross-platform corruption; `008f345` here committed 14 symlinks.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-15.md]] — Skills repo wrap-up session (13:42): worktree-routed edits due to the background-isolation guard, `.claude/worktrees/` four live worktrees and nested-repo hazard, explicit-path staging then `.gitignore` (`163db25`), commits `faf6be8` / `2c5a10a` / `008f345` / `163db25`, diff review plus credential scan before committing pre-existing work, two self-caught mistakes (`anthropic-skills` removal, 20 dropped trigger phrases), `mattpocock-skills` as the remaining lever on Codex's 171 excluded skills.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/subagent-driven-development.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Subagent-Driven Development (Two-Stage Review)\"\naliases: [Subagent-Driven Development skill, spec compliance reviewer, code quality reviewer, two-stage review process]\ntags: [claude-code, skills, development-methodology, code-review, subagents]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-24.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-24\nupdated: 2026-04-24\n---\n\n# Subagent-Driven Development (Two-Stage Review)\n\nSubagent-Driven Development is a Claude Code skill/methodology for implementing tasks from a written plan via subagents, followed by two independent review passes - a spec-compliance review and a separate code-quality review - before a task is considered done. It was used on 2026-04-24 to complete the final task of the [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]].\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Tasks are drawn from a written plan document (e.g., `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-04-24-perch-weight-room-domain.md`), with each task implemented by a subagent.\n- After implementation, a spec-compliance reviewer checks the result against the plan's stated requirements using a checklist; Task 9 passed this stage 12/12.\n- A second, separately-focused code-quality reviewer then reviews the same implementation for correctness and quality issues independent of the spec checklist.\n- On Task 9, the code-quality reviewer caught a real bug - two chart-cleanup arrays in `report.html.j2` missing a `\"trend-wr\"` entry - that had passed both the implementer's self-review and the spec-compliance review.\n- After the implementer fixed the flagged bug, the code-quality reviewer re-reviewed and passed it, completing the task.\n\n## Details\n\nThe methodology separates \"did this match the plan?\" from \"is this code actually correct and well-written?\" into two distinct review passes, each performed independently. This separation matters because a fully spec-compliant implementation can still contain bugs that the spec's acceptance checklist simply doesn't enumerate - in this case, the plan's requirements for Task 9 didn't explicitly call out every chart-cleanup array that needed a `\"trend-wr\"` entry, so the spec-compliance reviewer had nothing to flag even though it checked all 12 of its listed items. The code-quality reviewer, working from a different lens (general code correctness rather than a fixed checklist), caught the omission.\n\nThis two-stage structure is a development-process instance of a broader pattern also seen in data-pipeline work: a single review/verification pass, however thorough by its own criteria, can miss errors that a differently-focused second pass would catch. See [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] for how this Task 9 experience parallels an earlier citation-verification fix in [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]. Practically, the methodology produced a clean outcome for the [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]]: with Task 9's bug fixed and re-reviewed, the full 9-task feature reached 26/26 passing tests across 11 commits.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]] - The project whose final task (Task 9) was completed using this methodology.\n- [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] - Generalizes this two-stage review pattern alongside an earlier, differently-shaped verification lesson.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-24.md]] - Task 9 implementation and two-stage review session (17:06).\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 281) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-15.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-15\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (08:42)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` and `/dev-sync` on the Mac; resolved four backup errors and confirmed all repos clean.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Initial backup run: 4 errors, 78 jobs, CRASHBOX SSD mounted. Two were merge-needed repos (`dotclaude`, `global-memory` had new Windows commits) — merged and pushed. One was an unexplained `.codex` → SSD copy failure. One was 6 repos created on Windows yesterday missing from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`: `PP_Recruiting`, `PythonScripts_2024`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, `Supervisor_Reviews`, `VALD_Performance`, `VALD_Release`.\n- Agent recommended classifying the 6 as work repos based on Baylor-sounding names; user corrected: **they are personal**.\n- Sync pulled Windows commits into 5 repos: `_INBOX`, `agent-skills`, `CLI_Dev`, `LibraryProject`, `ZoteroInjestion`. No conflicts, no missing clones. `LibraryProject`'s vendored `ZoteroInjestion` copy was one commit behind and was fast-forwarded.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- 6 new repos added to `backup-manifest.json` as **personal**, destination SSD + GitHub (user overrode the work-repo suggestion). Committed and pushed.\n- Moved the 4 stale SSD skill folders to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` rather than deleting — copies also exist in the skills repo on GitHub.\n- Archived and cleared `.errors.log` only after a clean run (0 errors, 86 jobs); pushed.\n- User approved documenting the symlink-vs-directory fix in the `/backup` skill notes.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Root cause of the `.codex` SSD copy failure:** on the Mac, Codex skills `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout` are now **symlinks**, but the SSD backup still held them as real directories. rsync cannot replace an existing directory with a symlink, so the copy failed with no useful error. This recurs any time a skill converts to a symlink — stale real directories must be moved aside on the SSD.\n- Job-count sanity check confirmed again: SSD mounted → high job count (78, then 86); single-digit `okCount` with `status: OK` means the SSD was absent and working-tree capture silently skipped.\n- Backup manifest classification is not inferable from repo names — Baylor-sounding names can still be personal repos. Ask.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Add the symlink/stale-directory gotcha to the `/backup` skill** (user said yes) — document that `.codex` copy failures trace to real directories on the SSD shadowing new symlinks, and the fix is to move them to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-<date>/`.\n- `ZoteroInjestion` `docs/STATUS.md` has **no Next section** and still carries an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom — needs reconciling.\n- Optional cleanup: delete `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` later.\n- Carried over, still open: Windows backup fix (`FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md` scheduled-task triggers); confirm the 913-file skill audit was intentional.\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-16T20:15:24-05:00] compile | 2026-09-15.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-15.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-15.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"dequeue","timestamp":"2026-09-17T01:15:26.162Z","sessionId":"8d446553-e31c-429b-ac85-0d3ace208e37"}
{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2a1bb5dd-78aa-45a4-975a-eb80e47fc530","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a knowledge compiler. Your job is to read a daily conversation log\nand extract knowledge into structured wiki articles.\n\n## Schema (AGENTS.md)\n\n# AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema\n\n> Adapted from [Andrej Karpathy's LLM Knowledge Base](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f) architecture.\n> Instead of ingesting external articles, this system compiles knowledge from your own AI conversations.\n\n## The Compiler Analogy\n\n```\ndaily/          = source code    (your conversations - the raw material)\nLLM             = compiler       (extracts and organizes knowledge)\nknowledge/      = executable     (structured, queryable knowledge base)\nlint            = test suite     (health checks for consistency)\nmemlint         = test suite     (same, for the memory files loaded each session)\nqueries         = runtime        (using the knowledge)\n```\n\nYou don't manually organize your knowledge. You have conversations, and the LLM handles the synthesis, cross-referencing, and maintenance.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Layer 1: `daily/` - Conversation Logs (Immutable Source)\n\nDaily logs capture what happened in your AI coding sessions. These are the \"raw sources\" - append-only, never edited after the fact.\n\n```\ndaily/\n├── 2026-04-01.md\n├── 2026-04-02.md\n├── ...\n```\n\nEach file follows this format:\n\n```markdown\n# Daily Log: YYYY-MM-DD\n\n## Sessions\n\n### Session (HH:MM) - Brief Title\n\n**Context:** What the user was working on.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- User asked about X, assistant explained Y\n- Decided to use Z approach because...\n- Discovered that W doesn't work when...\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Chose library X over Y because...\n- Architecture: went with pattern Z\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Always do X before Y to avoid...\n- The gotcha with Z is that...\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [ ] Follow up on X\n- [ ] Refactor Y when time permits\n```\n\n### Layer 2: `knowledge/` - Compiled Knowledge (LLM-Owned)\n\nThe LLM owns this directory entirely. Humans read it but rarely edit it directly.\n\n```\nknowledge/\n├── index.md              # Master catalog - every article with one-line summary\n├── log.md                # Append-only chronological build log\n├── concepts/             # Atomic knowledge articles\n├── connections/          # Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n└── qa/                   # Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n```\n\n### Layer 3: This File (AGENTS.md)\n\nThe schema that tells the LLM how to compile and maintain the knowledge base. This is the \"compiler specification.\"\n\n---\n\n## Structural Files\n\n### `knowledge/index.md` - Master Catalog\n\nA table listing every knowledge article. This is the primary retrieval mechanism - the LLM reads this FIRST when answering any query, then selects relevant articles to read in full.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/supabase-auth]] | Row-level security patterns and JWT gotchas | daily/2026-04-02.md | 2026-04-02 |\n| [[connections/auth-and-webhooks]] | Token verification patterns shared across Supabase auth and Stripe webhooks | daily/2026-04-02.md, daily/2026-04-04.md | 2026-04-04 |\n```\n\n### `knowledge/log.md` - Build Log\n\nAppend-only chronological record of every compile, query, and lint operation.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Build Log\n\n## [2026-04-01T14:30:00] compile | Daily Log 2026-04-01\n- Source: daily/2026-04-01.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/nextjs-project-structure]], [[concepts/tailwind-setup]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n\n## [2026-04-02T09:00:00] query | \"How do I handle auth redirects?\"\n- Consulted: [[concepts/supabase-auth]], [[concepts/nextjs-middleware]]\n- Filed to: [[qa/auth-redirect-handling]]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Article Formats\n\n### Concept Articles (`knowledge/concepts/`)\n\nOne article per atomic piece of knowledge. These are facts, patterns, decisions, preferences, and lessons extracted from your conversations.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Concept Name\"\naliases: [alternate-name, abbreviation]\ntags: [domain, topic]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-01.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-03.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-01\nupdated: 2026-04-03\n---\n\n# Concept Name\n\n[2-4 sentence core explanation]\n\n## Key Points\n\n- [Bullet points, each self-contained]\n\n## Details\n\n[Deeper explanation, encyclopedia-style paragraphs]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/related-concept]] - How it connects\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-01.md]] - Initial discovery during project setup\n- [[daily/2026-04-03.md]] - Updated after debugging session\n```\n\n### Connection Articles (`knowledge/connections/`)\n\nCross-cutting synthesis linking 2+ concepts. Created when a conversation reveals a non-obvious relationship.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: X and Y\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/concept-x\"\n  - \"concepts/concept-y\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-04\nupdated: 2026-04-04\n---\n\n# Connection: X and Y\n\n## The Connection\n\n[What links these concepts]\n\n## Key Insight\n\n[The non-obvious relationship discovered]\n\n## Evidence\n\n[Specific examples from conversations]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/concept-x]]\n- [[concepts/concept-y]]\n```\n\n### Q&A Articles (`knowledge/qa/`)\n\nFiled answers from queries. Every complex question answered by the system can be permanently stored, making future queries smarter.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Q: Original Question\"\nquestion: \"The exact question asked\"\nconsulted:\n  - \"concepts/article-1\"\n  - \"concepts/article-2\"\nfiled: 2026-04-05\n---\n\n# Q: Original Question\n\n## Answer\n\n[The synthesized answer with [[wikilinks]] to sources]\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- [[concepts/article-1]] - Relevant because...\n- [[concepts/article-2]] - Provided context on...\n\n## Follow-Up Questions\n\n- What about edge case X?\n- How does this change if Y?\n```\n\n---\n\n## Core Operations\n\n### 1. Compile (daily/ -> knowledge/)\n\nWhen processing a daily log:\n\n1. Read the daily log file\n2. Read `knowledge/index.md` to understand current knowledge state\n3. Read existing articles that may need updating\n4. For each piece of knowledge found in the log:\n   - If an existing concept article covers this topic: UPDATE it with new information, add the daily log as a source\n   - If it's a new topic: CREATE a new `concepts/` article\n5. If the log reveals a non-obvious connection between 2+ existing concepts: CREATE a `connections/` article\n6. UPDATE `knowledge/index.md` with new/modified entries\n7. APPEND to `knowledge/log.md`\n\n**Important guidelines:**\n- A single daily log may touch 3-10 knowledge articles\n- Prefer updating existing articles over creating near-duplicates\n- Use Obsidian-style `[[wikilinks]]` with full relative paths from knowledge/\n- Write in encyclopedia style - factual, concise, self-contained\n- Every article must have YAML frontmatter\n- Every article must link back to its source daily logs\n\n### 2. Query (Ask the Knowledge Base)\n\n1. Read `knowledge/index.md` (the master catalog)\n2. Based on the question, identify 3-10 relevant articles from the index\n3. Read those articles in full\n4. Synthesize an answer with `[[wikilink]]` citations\n5. If `--file-back` is specified: create a `knowledge/qa/` article and update index.md and log.md\n\n**Why this works without RAG:** At personal knowledge base scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms cosine similarity. The LLM understands what the question is really asking and selects pages accordingly. Embeddings find similar words; the LLM finds relevant concepts.\n\n### 3. Lint (Health Checks)\n\nSeven checks, run periodically:\n\n1. **Broken links** - `[[wikilinks]]` pointing to non-existent articles\n2. **Orphan pages** - Articles with zero inbound links from other articles\n3. **Orphan sources** - Daily logs that haven't been compiled yet\n4. **Stale articles** - Source daily log changed since article was last compiled\n5. **Contradictions** - Conflicting claims across articles (requires LLM judgment)\n6. **Missing backlinks** - A links to B but B doesn't link back to A\n7. **Sparse articles** - Below 200 words, likely incomplete\n\nOutput: a markdown report with severity levels (error, warning, suggestion).\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Wikilinks:** Use Obsidian-style `[[path/to/article]]` without `.md` extension\n- **Writing style:** Encyclopedia-style, factual, third-person where appropriate\n- **Dates:** ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD for dates, full ISO for timestamps in log.md)\n- **File naming:** lowercase, hyphens for spaces (e.g., `supabase-row-level-security.md`)\n- **Frontmatter:** Every article must have YAML frontmatter with at minimum: title, sources, created, updated\n- **Sources:** Always link back to the daily log(s) that contributed to an article\n\n---\n\n## Full Project Structure\n\n```\nllm-personal-kb/\n|-- .claude/\n|   |-- settings.json                # Hook configuration (auto-activates in Claude Code)\n|-- .gitignore                       # Excludes runtime state, temp files, caches\n|-- AGENTS.md                        # This file - schema + full technical reference\n|-- README.md                        # Concise overview + quick start\n|-- pyproject.toml                   # Dependencies (at root so hooks can find it)\n|-- daily/                           # \"Source code\" - conversation logs (immutable)\n|-- knowledge/                       # \"Executable\" - compiled knowledge (LLM-owned)\n|   |-- index.md                     #   Master catalog - THE retrieval mechanism\n|   |-- log.md                       #   Append-only build log\n|   |-- concepts/                    #   Atomic knowledge articles\n|   |-- connections/                 #   Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n|   |-- qa/                          #   Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n|-- scripts/                         # CLI tools\n|   |-- compile.py                   #   Compile daily logs -> knowledge articles\n|   |-- query.py                     #   Ask questions (index-guided, no RAG)\n|   |-- lint.py                      #   7 health checks (knowledge base)\n|   |-- memlint.py                   #   6 health checks (memory tier)\n|   |-- flush.py                     #   Extract memories from conversations (background)\n|   |-- config.py                    #   Path constants\n|   |-- utils.py                     #   Shared helpers\n|-- hooks/                           # Claude Code hooks\n|   |-- session-start.py             #   Injects knowledge into every session\n|   |-- session-end.py               #   Extracts conversation -> daily log\n|   |-- pre-compact.py               #   Safety net: captures context before compaction\n|-- reports/                         # Lint reports (gitignored)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Hook System (Automatic Capture)\n\nHooks are configured in `.claude/settings.json` and fire automatically when you use Claude Code in this project.\n\n### `.claude/settings.json` Format\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"SessionStart\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-start.py\", \"timeout\": 15 }] }],\n    \"PreCompact\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/pre-compact.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }],\n    \"SessionEnd\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-end.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCommands use simple relative paths from the project root. Empty `matcher` catches all events.\n\n### Hook Details\n\n**`session-start.py`** (SessionStart)\n- Pure local I/O, no API calls, runs in under 1 second\n- Reads `knowledge/index.md` and the most recent daily log\n- Outputs JSON to stdout: `{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"...\"}}`\n- Claude sees the knowledge base index at the start of every session\n- Max context: 20,000 characters\n\n**`session-end.py`** (SessionEnd)\n- Reads hook input from stdin (JSON with `session_id`, `transcript_path`, `cwd`)\n- Copies the raw JSONL transcript to a temp file (no parsing in the hook - keeps it fast)\n- Spawns `flush.py` as a fully detached background process\n- Recursion guard: exits immediately if `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` env var is set\n\n**`pre-compact.py`** (PreCompact)\n- Same architecture as session-end.py\n- Fires before Claude Code auto-compacts the context window\n- Guards against empty `transcript_path` (known Claude Code bug #13668)\n- Critical for long sessions: captures context before summarization discards it\n\n**Why both PreCompact and SessionEnd?** Long-running sessions may trigger multiple auto-compactions before you close the session. Without PreCompact, intermediate context is lost to summarization before SessionEnd ever fires.\n\n### Background Flush Process (`flush.py`)\n\nSpawned by both hooks as a fully detached background process:\n- **Windows:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | DETACHED_PROCESS` flags\n- **Mac/Linux:** `start_new_session=True`\n\nThis ensures flush.py survives after Claude Code's hook process exits.\n\n**What flush.py does:**\n1. Sets `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY=memory_flush` env var (prevents recursive hook firing)\n2. Reads the pre-extracted conversation context from the temp `.md` file\n3. Skips if context is empty or if same session was flushed within 60 seconds (deduplication)\n4. Calls Claude Agent SDK (`query()` with `allowed_tools=[]`, `max_turns=2`)\n5. Claude decides what's worth saving - returns structured bullet points or `FLUSH_OK`\n6. Appends result to `daily/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n7. Cleans up temp context file\n8. **End-of-day auto-compilation:** If it's past 6 PM local time (`COMPILE_AFTER_HOUR = 18`) and today's daily log has changed since its last compilation (hash comparison against `state.json`), spawns `compile.py` as another detached background process. This means compilation happens automatically once a day without needing a cron job or manual trigger.\n\n### JSONL Transcript Format\n\nClaude Code stores conversations as `.jsonl` files. Messages are nested under a `message` key:\n\n```python\nentry = json.loads(line)\nmsg = entry.get(\"message\", {})\nrole = msg.get(\"role\", \"\")     # \"user\" or \"assistant\"\ncontent = msg.get(\"content\", \"\")  # string or list of content blocks\n```\n\nContent can be a string or a list of blocks (`{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}` dicts).\n\n---\n\n## Script Details\n\n### compile.py - The Compiler\n\nUses the Claude Agent SDK's async streaming `query()`:\n\n```python\nasync for message in query(\n    prompt=compile_prompt,\n    options=ClaudeAgentOptions(\n        cwd=str(ROOT_DIR),\n        system_prompt={\"type\": \"preset\", \"preset\": \"claude_code\"},\n        allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Glob\", \"Grep\"],\n        permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",\n        max_turns=30,\n    ),\n):\n```\n\n- Builds a prompt with: AGENTS.md schema, current index, all existing articles, and the daily log\n- Claude reads the daily log, decides what concepts to extract, and writes files directly\n- `permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\"` auto-approves all file operations\n- Incremental: tracks SHA-256 hashes of daily logs in `state.json`, skips unchanged files\n- Cost: ~$0.45-0.65 per daily log (increases as KB grows)\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/compile.py              # compile new/changed only\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --all        # force recompile everything\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --file daily/2026-04-01.md\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --dry-run\n```\n\n### query.py - Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nLoads the entire knowledge base into context (index + all articles). No RAG.\n\nAt personal KB scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms vector similarity. The LLM understands what you're really asking; cosine similarity just finds similar words.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What auth patterns do I use?\"\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What's my error handling strategy?\" --file-back\n```\n\nWith `--file-back`, creates a Q&A article in `knowledge/qa/` and updates the index and log. This is the compounding loop - every question makes the KB smarter.\n\n### lint.py - Health Checks\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[wikilinks]]` to non-existent articles |\n| Orphan pages | Structural | Articles with zero inbound links |\n| Orphan sources | Structural | Daily logs not yet compiled |\n| Stale articles | Structural | Source logs changed since compilation |\n| Missing backlinks | Structural | A links to B but B doesn't link back |\n| Sparse articles | Structural | Under 200 words |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting claims across articles |\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/lint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/lint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/lint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`.\n\n### memlint.py - Memory Tier Health Checks\n\n`lint.py` stops at `knowledge/`. The files the harness actually injects into every\nsession live elsewhere and were never audited: `~/.claude/global-memory/` and the\nactive `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` slugs. Drift there is the most expensive\ndrift in the system, because it is read at the start of every conversation.\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Index drift | Structural | Memory file missing from MEMORY.md, or an index pointer with no file |\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[links]]` that do not match any frontmatter `name:` in the slug |\n| Name form | Structural | `name:` that is not the bare kebab stem (type belongs in `metadata.type`) |\n| Near-duplicates | Structural | Within-slug TF-IDF similarity above the threshold |\n| Cap overruns | Structural | A file past the character cap it declares in its own header |\n| Content in index | Structural | Facts written into MEMORY.md, which is meant to hold pointers |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting, redundant, or superseded claims across memory files |\n\n**Scope.** Retired `C--Users-eric-rash*` slugs are excluded everywhere - the macOS\nslugs were re-keyed from them, so including them reports history as duplication.\n`handoffs/` and `transcripts/` are session records, not memory, and are never linted.\nThe LLM check defaults to `global-memory` + the `DEV` and `~` slugs, the ones that\nload together in a working session; `--all-slugs` widens it.\n\n**Similarity threshold.** Default 0.45. Measured 2026-09-01: the highest genuine\nwithin-slug pair scored 0.33. The report always prints the top pairs per slug so\nthe number can be retuned against evidence.\n\n**Report only.** memlint never edits a memory file. Merging and deleting memories\nstays a human decision.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --all-slugs        # widen the LLM check\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --threshold 0.6    # retune near-duplicates\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/memlint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`; `--structural-only` writes\n`memlint-YYYY-MM-DD-structural.md` instead, so a free run never clobbers the\nday's paid-for LLM findings. Runs automatically after the Sunday 02:30\nadjudication job.\n\n---\n\n## State Tracking\n\n`scripts/state.json` tracks:\n- `ingested` - map of daily log filenames to SHA-256 hashes, compilation timestamps, and costs\n- `query_count` - total queries run\n- `last_lint` - timestamp of most recent lint\n- `last_memlint` - timestamp of most recent memory-tier lint\n- `total_cost` - cumulative API cost\n\n`scripts/last-flush.json` tracks flush deduplication (session_id + timestamp).\n\nBoth are gitignored and regenerated automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies\n\n`pyproject.toml` (at project root):\n- `claude-agent-sdk>=0.1.29` - Claude Agent SDK for LLM calls with tool use\n- `python-dotenv>=1.0.0` - Environment variable management\n- `tzdata>=2024.1` - Timezone data\n- Python 3.12+, managed by [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)\n\nNo API key needed - uses Claude Code's built-in credentials at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n\n---\n\n## Costs\n\n| Operation | Cost |\n|-----------|------|\n| Compile one daily log | $0.45-0.65 |\n| Query (no file-back) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Query (with file-back) | ~$0.25-0.40 |\n| Full lint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural lint only | $0.00 |\n| Full memlint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural memlint only | $0.00 |\n| Memory flush (per session) | ~$0.02-0.05 |\n\n---\n\n## Customization\n\n### Additional Article Types\n\nAdd directories like `people/`, `projects/`, `tools/` to `knowledge/`. Define the article format in this file (AGENTS.md) and update `utils.py`'s `list_wiki_articles()` to include them.\n\n### Obsidian Integration\n\nThe knowledge base is pure markdown with `[[wikilinks]]` - works natively in Obsidian. Point a vault at `knowledge/` for graph view, backlinks, and search.\n\n### Scaling Beyond Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nAt ~2,000+ articles / ~2M+ tokens, the index becomes too large for the context window. At that point, add hybrid RAG (keyword + semantic search) as a retrieval layer before the LLM. See Karpathy's recommendation of `qmd` by Tobi Lutke for search at scale.\n\n\n## Current Wiki Index\n\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] | Adds 2026-07-09 GitHub MCP 401-on-reconnect: configured Authorization header disables OAuth fallback; fix is token refresh or header removal | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-03.md, 2026-06-09.md, 2026-07-09.md | 2026-08-01 |\n| [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] | Three new skills (web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system) built for the SecondBrain vault; now cross-linked to an earlier Obsidian-vault-as-destination decision | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] | Lean-mode on-ramp pipeline; now includes 2026-06-05 sparse-input run and cumulative ctx savings (4.0 MB / 22 days / 105 conversations, 214× compression before /compact) | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-05.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] | Local Catapult Connect v6 API reference; now cross-linked to a mirror-image Perch API auth-scheme lesson from 2026-04-25 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project]] | KeystoneOS Tezino Phase 1 data-gap closure (CMJ/IMTP backfill, mRSI decline, Catapult token failure); now cross-linked to downstream Tezino_CampPrep SPC monitoring project initiated 2026-06-09 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-09.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[connections/catapult-api-docs-and-tezino-token-issue]] | Same-day link between the Catapult API reference's token docs and the Tezino sync's stale-token failure | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick]] | Gap between the new web-scraping skill's routing tiers and the ReadMe .md/llms.txt technique that actually worked | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] | Memory compiler setup; now includes version-skew failure mode (claude-agent-sdk bundled CLI diverges from system), fix via uv lock --upgrade-package, and Tier B independence confirmed | 2026-06-13.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment]] | Baylor Load Simulator Vercel deployment; now includes the 2026-05-12 Comparison View feature and Catapult period-aggregation sync findings | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-12.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports]] | CMJ return-to-competition HTML reports for athlete Skylar Zinnecker, built from a VALD ForceDecks export, with redefined baseline and \"Post-Op Low\" terminology | 2026-04-14.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] | Recurring Catapult OpenField token friction linking the Baylor Load Simulator deploy, the Tezino sync failure, and the Catapult API reference | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects]] | Shared VALD ForceDecks CMJ data and mRSI metric linking Skylar Zinnecker's RTC reports to the Tezino narrative backfill | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]] | Round-7 batch import + data-quality repair; now records the \"verify prior background agents actually finished\" procedural lesson from 2026-04-22 | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/graphify-vault-separation]] | Decision to move graphify's 300+ generated files out of SportScienceResearchRepo and into the user's Obsidian vault | 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] | Obsidian SecondBrain as recurring knowledge destination: graphify cleanup, /ingest-resource skill, morning briefing durable copy, and Applied Performance OKR hub as canonical project home | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-06-10.md, 2026-05-21.md, 2026-06-01.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]] | \"The Monitor\" newsletter Issue 01 production: content verification, staff photo integration, preview-server rendering gotcha, and template-directory documentation | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] | Links a 2026-04-22 agent-citation-hallucination fix and a newsletter photo-rendering gotcha as instances of \"verify directly, don't trust the intermediate layer\" | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]] | Perch/Weight Room domain feature, now including its 2026-04-25 data-activation completion (436 athletes, 12,300 set records ingested via corrected Perch API integration) | 2026-04-24.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] | Two-stage review methodology (spec-compliance then code-quality) used to implement and verify Perch/Weight Room Task 9 | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]] | Jinja2's `default()` filter unreliably handles `None`; use explicit `is not none` guards instead | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] | Multi-stage review principle extended to planning context: spec self-review catching carryover resolution behavioral gap | 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] | Verified Perch API integration details (Bearer auth, /v3/sets pagination, 1RM formula, exercise/org IDs) that completed the Weight Room domain's data-activation step | 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards]] | Links a 2026-04-25 Perch auth-scheme surprise (Bearer worked, JWT didn't) to the 2026-06-10 Catapult token failure as mirror-image \"credential appearance is unreliable\" lessons | 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-comparison-view]] | Projected-vs-Actual ComparisonChart on /practices/[id]/compare, planned /comparison nav tab, single-metric scope; now cross-linked to the Color Triad Intended↔Realized leg | 2026-05-12.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/catapu