t_time","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__update_event","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__copy_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__create_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__download_file_content","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_metadata","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__get_file_permissions","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__list_recent_files","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__read_file_content","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__search_files","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__share_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__trash_file","mcp__claude_ai_Google_Drive__update_file","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__compose","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get-design-import-job-status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get-send-to-hyperframes-guide","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_project","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_project_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__get_render_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__import-claude-design-from-url","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__internal_get_project_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__internal_get_render_status","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__list_projects","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__render_video","mcp__claude_ai_HyperFrames_by_HeyGen__submit_feedback"],"removedNames":[],"readdedNames":[],"pendingMcpServers":[],"needsAuthMcpServers":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"703f4fe6-0e27-45fb-b039-a583cf4e3419","timestamp":"2026-09-17T01:15:27.441Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"8d446553-e31c-429b-ac85-0d3ace208e37","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"703f4fe6-0e27-45fb-b039-a583cf4e3419","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"agent_listing_delta","addedTypes":["claude","Explore","general-purpose","Plan","statusline-setup"],"addedLines":["- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)","- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)","- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)"],"removedTypes":[],"isInitial":true,"showConcurrencyNote":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9cfa1061-237c-47cc-846a-e5d2dfff502e","timestamp":"2026-09-17T01:15:27.441Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"8d446553-e31c-429b-ac85-0d3ace208e37","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"9cfa1061-237c-47cc-846a-e5d2dfff502e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"mcp_instructions_delta","addedNames":["claude.ai Claude Docs","claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen"],"addedBlocks":["## claude.ai Claude Docs\nClaude Docs: living docs you create and edit here. A docs skill your client lists → load it before any docs call — also before a `read`, comment or tab change on a claude.ai …/artifact/… link (the link is a doc; never web-fetch it). No docs skill or guide text loaded → `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )` alone before any docs call but a doc's birth. Make a doc here — not a local file, even when coding — only when the user asks for one, and make it FIRST: the turn's first tool call is its skeleton (title, byline, a `pending` block per section) — a reflex: send it before any search, file read, plan, `guide` or thinking it through; think once it is open — `batch( container = {\"kind\":\"project\",\"create\":{\"name\":\"<title>\",\"doc\":{\"blocks\":{\"asof\":{\"type\":\"date\",\"value\":\"<today>\"},\"me\":{\"type\":\"mention\",\"user\":\"me\"},\"s1\":{\"type\":\"pending\",\"intent\":\"Goals: the three outcomes this quarter commits to\"},\"s2\":{…}},\"markdown\":\"# <title>\\n\\n<?claude block asof?> · <?claude block me?>\\n\\n<?claude block s1?>\\n\\n<?claude block s2?>\"}}}, batch = [] )` (`<?claude block k?>` ↔ `blocks.k`); its ack links the doc → `open` it with your Artifact tool (none → start your next message with the link, once); they're likely watching it fill — keep them posted in a short line naming what you're on (outline up; now <topic>); findings go in the doc, not chat; then `guide( items = [\"topic.index\"] )`, research, and fill each section: `replace` its pending id with `## <heading>` + body; end with one line + the link, never the document. Summoned by a doc comment (turn headed `[Artifact comment sent to Claude]`, `;thread=<root id>`): answer ONLY with a doc comment under that root (`create` an utterance, parent `<root id>`) — no artifact/platform comment tool: that relay thread is resolved and never reaches the doc; an edit asked there → `update` with `answering: \"<root id>\"`.","## claude.ai HyperFrames by HeyGen\n\n[HeyGen HyperFrames MCP] — Programmable HTML video projects.\n\n## When to use this MCP\n\nThis MCP is the primary HyperFrames surface for hosted chat clients (Claude.ai\nweb/desktop, ChatGPT, Grok, and similar), where the user has no local filesystem\nand the agent cannot author HyperFrames code directly.\n\nFrom a CLI/IDE agent on the user's machine (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, the\nlocalhost MCP Inspector, etc.), **`compose` and `render_video` are disabled** — these clients have a local\nfilesystem, so author HyperFrames with the local HyperFrames skills instead, which\nproduce standalone HTML compositions the user can edit, diff, and commit. Calls to\nthose two tools from such clients are rejected with a pointer to install them:\n\n    npx skills add heygen-com/hyperframes\n\nThe read tools (list_projects, get_project, get_project_status, get_render_status)\nstay available to every client.\n\nAfter installation they expose slash commands like `/hyperframes` (compose),\n`/hyperframes-cli` (init, lint, preview, render), and `/hyperframes-media`\n(TTS, transcribe, remove-background). See\nhttps://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes for the full skill list and docs.\n\nBut the skills and this MCP are *not* drop-in equivalents:\n\n- Local skills → standalone HTML/GSAP files on disk. No `project_id`, no hosted\n  artifact, no integrated cloud render pipeline.\n- This MCP → hosted HeyGen project with a `project_id`, a live canvas at\n  `app.heygen.com/projects/{id}`, and the cloud render queue.\n\nRoute by what the user actually wants:\n- \"Make a local HTML composition / something I can edit\" → use a local skill.\n- \"Make a HeyGen project I can share / render in the cloud / open in the app\"\n  → use this MCP, even from a CLI agent.\n\n## What it does\n\nHyperFrames is HeyGen's framework for creating, editing, and rendering programmable\nHTML-based video projects. Projects are authored interactively (Magic Edit\nchat-driven flow) and rendered to MP4, WebM, or MOV at any framerate. Tools exposed\nhere cover composing, listing, fetching, and rendering projects… [truncated]"],"removedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"52faf1b6-fd90-40ac-aa01-8cea39e1a0f3","timestamp":"2026-09-17T01:15:27.441Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"8d446553-e31c-429b-ac85-0d3ace208e37","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"52faf1b6-fd90-40ac-aa01-8cea39e1a0f3","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch","skillCount":12,"isInitial":true,"names":["dataviz","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","run","init","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"21a6755c-e3db-4fac-af24-3acad6b5f64c","timestamp":"2026-09-17T01:15:27.441Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"8d446553-e31c-429b-ac85-0d3ace208e37","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"type":"last-prompt","lastPrompt":"You are a knowledge compiler. Your job is to read a daily conversation log and extract knowledge into structured wiki articles.  ## Schema (AGENTS.md)  # AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema  >…","leafUuid":"21a6755c-e3db-4fac-af24-3acad6b5f64c","sessionId":"8d446553-e31c-429b-ac85-0d3ace208e37"}
{"parentUuid":"21a6755c-e3db-4fac-af24-3acad6b5f64c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf88K4cWadfHpk2FAP4uE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"===ARTICLE concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory.md===\n---\ntitle: \"rsync Cannot Replace a Directory With a Symlink: Stale SSD Skill Folders\"\naliases: [.codex SSD copy failure, symlink vs directory rsync, _OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15, dev-sync new-project startup closeout symlinks, stale real directories shadowing symlinks]\ntags: [backup, rsync, symlinks, macos, skills, gotcha, maintenance]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-15\nupdated: 2026-09-15\n---\n\n# rsync Cannot Replace a Directory With a Symlink: Stale SSD Skill Folders\n\nOne of four errors in the 2026-09-15 `/backup` run was an unexplained `.codex` → CRASHBOX SSD copy failure with no useful error message. The root cause: four Codex skills — `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout` — are now **symlinks** on the Mac, while the SSD backup still held them as real directories. rsync refuses to replace an existing destination directory with a symlink, so the copy failed silently in the sense that mattered. The fix was to move the four stale directories to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/`, after which a re-run completed with 0 errors and 86 jobs.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The failure is a type change at the source, not a content change.** A skill converting from a real directory to a symlink is invisible locally and produces no local breakage; the damage is entirely at the mirror destination.\n- **rsync declines the directory → symlink substitution** rather than clearing the destination, so the failure is deterministic and repeats on every run until the stale directory is moved aside.\n- **The error text was unhelpful**, which is why the diagnosis had to come from comparing the source's file *type* against the destination's — not from reading the message.\n- **Remediation was a move, not a delete:** the four directories were relocated to a dated `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` folder, justified by the fact that equivalent copies also exist in the skills repo on GitHub.\n- **The gotcha was approved for documentation in the `/backup` skill**, so the next occurrence is a lookup rather than a re-diagnosis.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the second recorded instance of the same mechanism in four days. On 2026-09-12 a submodule conversion turned `LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion`'s `.git` from a directory into a file, and rsync refused that substitution too — documented in [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]]. The two cases differ only in which type change occurred (directory → file there, directory → symlink here) and in what caused it (a git operation there, a skills-layout refactor here). The shared shape is that a mirroring tool classifies by filesystem type where human intuition classifies by name and contents: to the operator, `~/.codex/skills/startup` is \"the startup skill\" before and after the conversion, while to rsync it is a different kind of object entirely. That recurrence is exactly the signal described in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — a second occurrence of a failure class means the durable fix belongs in the tooling or its documentation, not in another one-off cleanup, which is why writing it into the `/backup` skill notes was the right disposition.\n\nThe unhelpful error message is worth isolating as a diagnostic lesson. When a copy fails with no informative text, the instinct is to suspect permissions, disk space, or a transient I/O fault — none of which point toward comparing types. The discriminating check here is cheap and specific: list the source and destination entries and compare what kind of object each is. A destination that is a directory where the source is now a symlink (or a file) explains a failure that no amount of permission or capacity investigation would.\n\nThe remediation choice follows the reversibility discipline recurring across this backup stack. Moving 4 stale directories aside costs some SSD space and buys the ability to undo; deleting them would have been safe here because the skills repo on GitHub holds copies, but that redundancy was *verified* rather than assumed, matching the reasoning applied to the 139 MB `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` rename and to the Task 2 skills prune executed as a move. The dated folder name also inherits the once-daily-collision hazard catalogued in [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]]: a second symlink-conversion cleanup on the same date would target the same path.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] — The same rsync type-change refusal three days earlier, with `.git` going directory → file.\n- [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] — The general pattern this is now the third member of.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The `/backup` skill whose notes now carry this gotcha.\n- [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — The symlink topology that produced the type change in the first place.\n- [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] — A neighbouring symlink-depth failure in the same skills backup surface.\n- [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — Why a second occurrence redirects the fix into the tooling.\n- [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] — The date-keyed-path collision hazard `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` inherits.\n- [[concepts/pre-destructive-git-backup-discipline]] — The move-don't-delete reversibility instinct applied again.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-15.md]] — `/backup` session (08:42): unexplained `.codex` → SSD copy failure among four errors; root cause identified as Codex skills `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout` now being symlinks while the SSD held real directories, with rsync unable to replace a directory with a symlink; four stale folders moved to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` rather than deleted because the skills repo on GitHub holds copies; user approved documenting the fix in the `/backup` skill notes; clean re-run at 0 errors / 86 jobs.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Repo Classification Is Not Inferable From Names: Six Personal Repos That Sound Like Work\"\naliases: [PP_Recruiting VALD_Performance personal not work, backup manifest work vs personal classification, Baylor-sounding names personal repos, six new Windows repos manifest, ask don't infer classification]\ntags: [backup, manifest, classification, verification, cross-machine, macos, keystoneos]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-15\nupdated: 2026-09-15\n---\n\n# Repo Classification Is Not Inferable From Names: Six Personal Repos That Sound Like Work\n\nThe 2026-09-15 `/backup` run flagged six repositories created on the Windows machine the previous day as missing from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`: `PP_Recruiting`, `PythonScripts_2024`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, `Supervisor_Reviews`, `VALD_Performance`, `VALD_Release`. The agent proposed classifying all six as **work** repositories on the strength of their Baylor-sounding names. The user corrected this: they are **personal**. All six were added to the manifest as personal with destination SSD + GitHub, then committed and pushed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The inference was wrong in the direction that matters.** `VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, and `Supervisor_Reviews` all name Baylor-domain concepts, yet the repositories are personal — the vocabulary of a person's work naturally appears in their personal projects.\n- **Classification is a routing decision with consequences.** The work/personal axis governs backup destination and retention, so a misclassification silently sends data to the wrong place and is not self-correcting.\n- **The correct procedure is to ask, not to guess.** A name is a label chosen for the author's convenience; it carries no reliable signal about ownership or governance.\n- **Six repos appearing at once traces to a cross-machine burst** — all created on Windows the day before, which is the same bulk-creation event that outruns a hand-maintained manifest.\n- **Resolution was to add all six** rather than triage, following the same all-in disposition used when 49 folders were found unmanifested.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is a specific case of a rule already established for filenames and flags but not previously stated for manifest classification: the cheap identifier and the actual artifact are different things. [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] collects the cases where repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports each over- and under-included at once; a repo name is exactly that kind of cheap identifier, useful as a work queue entry and useless as a verdict. What makes classification a sharper instance than most is the asymmetry of the error. A wrong *liveness* guess gets corrected at the next run; a wrong *classification* is a persistent routing decision that keeps sending data somewhere it should not go, and nothing downstream will ever flag it, because the manifest is the authority the checks read from.\n\nThe professional-vocabulary overlap is the underlying reason the heuristic fails here in particular. Someone who works in athletic performance names their personal projects with the vocabulary of athletic performance — VALD, RTP, recruiting — because those are the domain terms they think in. A classifier reading only names therefore sees maximum signal at precisely the point where the signal is least informative. The counterpart error would also be possible: a genuinely work-owned repo named `scratch` or `tools` would be classified personal by the same heuristic with equal confidence.\n\nThe manifest-drift mechanism itself is now well documented. Six folders created in one day on a second machine is the same bulk-creation event that produced the 49-folder gap recorded in [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]], and it strengthens the standing argument in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] that the manifest should be derived from a scan rather than maintained by hand. Notably, auto-derivation would solve *coverage* while leaving *classification* untouched — a scanner can enumerate every folder but cannot decide whether `VALD_Release` is work or personal. That residue is the part that must stay a human answer, which makes \"ask\" not a temporary workaround but the permanent shape of this step.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The 49-folder instance of the same bulk-creation drift, with the same add-all-of-them resolution.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the six folders, and the auto-derivation proposal that would not cover classification.\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] — The general rule this extends: a name is a work-queue entry, not a verdict.\n- [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] — The Windows machine's activity that produced six new repos overnight.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — Why unmanifested folders are invisible to the error count.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same \"read inside rather than judge by name\" discipline applied to repo visibility.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-15.md]] — `/backup` session (08:42): six repos created on Windows the previous day (`PP_Recruiting`, `PythonScripts_2024`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, `Supervisor_Reviews`, `VALD_Performance`, `VALD_Release`) missing from `backup-manifest.json`; agent recommended classifying them as work repos based on Baylor-sounding names and the user corrected that they are personal; all six added as personal with destination SSD + GitHub, committed and pushed; explicit lesson that backup manifest classification is not inferable from repo names and must be asked.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count.md===\n---\ntitle: \"status: OK Is Not Proof of a Full Backup — Read okCount\"\naliases: [okCount cross-check, status OK insufficient, backup job count baseline drift, error count cannot detect missing SSD, 9 vs 31 vs 76 vs 86 jobs, two independent completeness axes]\ntags: [backup, automation, verification, macos, monitoring, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-15\n---\n\n# status: OK Is Not Proof of a Full Backup — Read okCount\n\nThe KeystoneOS daily backup reports a `status` field and an `okCount`, and the 2026-09-09 session restated as explicit policy that the former is insufficient on its own. A run can report `status: OK` with zero errors while having captured only a fraction of the intended data, because the error count measures the outcome of attempted jobs and cannot detect a job that was never attempted. The cross-check is `okCount`: a single-digit count means the CRASHBOX SSD was absent and only repo pushes plus Google Drive snapshots ran, while a high count means the working-tree capture leg actually executed.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The error count is structurally blind to a missing target.** An unmounted SSD removes whole job classes from the run; every remaining job succeeds, so the aggregate verdict is clean and wrong.\n- **`okCount` is the completeness signal, `status` is the execution signal.** They answer different questions and both must be read.\n- **The expected job count is not a constant.** It has moved repeatedly — roughly 31 for a full run in early September, 76 after 49 manifest entries were added on 09-09, and 78 then 86 on 09-15 as six more repos were added and four errors cleared. Any remembered number expires.\n- **Because the number drifts, the durable form of the check is the shape, not the threshold:** single-digit `okCount` alongside `status: OK` means the SSD was absent and working-tree capture silently skipped.\n- **There are two independent completeness axes:** whether the SSD was mounted (job classes present or absent) and whether the manifest is complete (folders in scope or not). A run can be full on one axis and empty on the other.\n\n## Details\n\nThis lesson has now been learned from three directions, and the pairing is what makes it durable. The 2026-09-04 finding recorded in [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] arrived at the job count from the hardware side: a charge-only USB-C cable left the CRASHBOX SSD unmounted, the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` job classes silently did not exist, and a clean run saved nothing that was uncommitted, non-repo, or gitignored. The 2026-09-09 finding arrives from the manifest side: the SSD was fine, but 49 folders were never in the instruction set, so their absence was equally invisible to the error count. In both cases the failure is that the denominator shrank rather than the numerator falling, which no ratio-free success indicator can express — the same defect described generally in [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]].\n\nThe baseline drift is the practical complication, and 2026-09-15 supplied the sharpest demonstration of it. On 2026-09-07 the tell was hardened by writing the *mechanism* into `SKILL.md` — the `CAPTURE` and `STATE-SSD` classes exist only when the drive is mounted — precisely so the check would not depend on remembering that 31 is good. Within eight days the full-run count had passed through 31, 76, 78, and 86 as manifest entries were added and errors cleared, which would have invalidated a memorized threshold three times over. The 09-15 session confirmed the check in its robust form: the SSD was mounted, the count was high, and the sanity check passed on the *contrast* between single-digit and high rather than on any particular figure. The generalizable rule, stated in [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]], is that a quantity used as a health check should be recorded at whatever abstraction level is stable.\n\nThe correct reading of a backup run is therefore three-part — did the attempted jobs succeed (`status`), were the SSD-dependent job classes present (`okCount` shape), and does the manifest still cover every folder that matters (`manifest-unmanaged` entries) — with no single field standing in for the other two. The 09-15 run exercised all three: four errors on the first pass (two merge-needed repos, one rsync type-change failure, one manifest gap), and a clean 86-job run only after each was addressed.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The origin of the job-count tell and its `CAPTURE`/`STATE-SSD` mechanism, discovered from the unmounted-drive side.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The manifest-side instance which also reset the expected job count from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] — The 09-15 manifest gap that moved the count again, and the classification question a scan cannot answer.\n- [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] — One of the four errors cleared between the 78-job and 86-job runs.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — A sibling case where a different backup status field answered a narrower question than the operator's.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general pattern: an accurate field reporting on a narrower predicate than the one being asked.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — Why a clean verdict over a shrunken work set is the recurring shape here.\n- [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]] — Why the tell was written as a mechanism rather than a threshold, validated three times over by the baseline changes.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): explicit lesson that `status: OK` alone is not proof of a full backup and `okCount` must be cross-checked (~31 jobs = SSD captured, ~9–10 = SSD absent with only repo pushes and Drive snapshots), that the error count cannot detect a missing SSD, and that manifest coverage is a parallel silent failure mode; job count moved 28 → 76 in the same session.\n- [[daily/2026-09-15.md]] — `/backup` session (08:42): job-count sanity check confirmed again with the SSD mounted (78 jobs on the first pass, 86 after fixes), and the durable form restated as single-digit `okCount` with `status: OK` meaning the SSD was absent and working-tree capture silently skipped.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Archive and Clear .errors.log Only After a Verified Clean Run\"\naliases: [clear errors log after clean run, archive then clear ordering, 0 errors 86 jobs before clearing, error log ordering discipline]\ntags: [backup, verification, logging, macos, maintenance, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-15\nupdated: 2026-09-15\n---\n\n# Archive and Clear .errors.log Only After a Verified Clean Run\n\nIn the 2026-09-15 `/backup` session, the `.errors.log` was archived and cleared **only after** a re-run completed with 0 errors and 86 jobs — not after the individual fixes were applied. The sequence was: diagnose four errors, fix all four, re-run to a clean result, then archive and clear, then push. This ordering makes the clear an assertion backed by evidence rather than an assertion of intent.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The clear is a claim about current state,** so it must be preceded by a fresh observation of that state, not by the belief that the fixes worked.\n- **Fixing and verifying are different events.** Four fixes were applied (two merges, one stale-directory move, one manifest addition); only the clean 86-job re-run established that all four took effect together.\n- **`.errors.log` is an escalation channel, not a state record** — the standing discipline that entries must be confirmed stale before removal, here satisfied by an independent clean run rather than by reasoning.\n- **The archive-then-clear step retains the evidence,** so a later question about what the four errors were remains answerable.\n- **The clear also silences the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning,** which is why premature clearing is tempting: the nag is immediate and the verification is not.\n\n## Details\n\nThe discipline in [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] establishes *that* entries must be confirmed stale against an independent source before clearing. This session supplies the operational ordering for the common case where the operator has just fixed the errors themselves: the independent source is a fresh run, and the fixes do not count as their own confirmation. The distinction matters because a multi-error run has cross-dependencies that reasoning can miss. Here, two of the four errors (merge-needed repos) were fixed by pushing, one (the rsync type-change failure) by moving stale directories aside, and one (the manifest gap) by editing and committing the manifest — and the manifest edit itself produced new work for the next run. A clear issued after the last fix would have been asserting that four independently-applied changes composed correctly, which is precisely the composition that [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] shows cannot be assumed from per-stage success.\n\nThe temptation to clear early comes from the asymmetry of feedback. The stale entry produces a visible HOOK FAILURE warning at every session start, so it is a continuous small cost, while the verification run is a discrete larger one. That asymmetry reliably pushes toward clearing on the strength of intent, which converts the log from a record of unresolved conditions into a record of conditions the operator *believed* resolved — the exact degradation that makes an escalation channel untrustworthy.\n\nThe archive half of the step carries its own known hazard. As documented in [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]], a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior log lines when a second clear happened on the same day; the archive is only a safety net if its name cannot collide. In this session the archive preceded the clear and the run was the day's only clear, so the net held — but the general form of the rule is that both halves need verification: verify the run before clearing, and verify the archive landed before trusting it as the record.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/stale-error-log-archival-discipline]] — The parent discipline; this article supplies the ordering for the self-fixed case.\n- [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] — The archive-side failure that makes the preservation step itself something to verify.\n- [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] — The general rule both halves instantiate.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The `/backup` skill that automates the archive-then-clear step.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — What \"clean run\" must mean before it can license a clear: status *and* job-count shape.\n- [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] — Why per-fix success does not compose into end-to-end assurance.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-15.md]] — `/backup` session (08:42): four errors diagnosed and fixed (two merge-needed repos, one `.codex` rsync type-change failure, one six-repo manifest gap), with `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a clean run at 0 errors and 86 jobs, then pushed.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Vendored Copy Drift: LibraryProject's Embedded ZoteroInjestion One Commit Behind\"\naliases: [LibraryProject vendored ZoteroInjestion behind, fast-forward vendored copy, STATUS.md no Next section, unmerged Windows copy pasted at bottom, two locations one project]\ntags: [git, sync, cross-machine, submodule, documentation, maintenance, keystoneos]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-15\nupdated: 2026-09-15\n---\n\n# Vendored Copy Drift: LibraryProject's Embedded ZoteroInjestion One Commit Behind\n\nThe 2026-09-15 `/dev-sync` run pulled Windows commits into five repositories (`_INBOX`, `agent-skills`, `CLI_Dev`, `LibraryProject`, `ZoteroInjestion`) with no conflicts and no missing clones. One substantive finding: `LibraryProject`'s vendored copy of `ZoteroInjestion` was one commit behind the standalone repo and had to be fast-forwarded separately. A related documentation defect was left open — `ZoteroInjestion`'s `docs/STATUS.md` has no Next section and still carries an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The same project existing at two paths means two sync operations.** Pulling `ZoteroInjestion` standalone does not advance the copy embedded in `LibraryProject`; each must be brought forward on its own.\n- **The drift was one commit and benign,** but it is silent by construction — nothing in a standalone pull reports that an embedded copy exists elsewhere and is now behind.\n- **This is the same repository already carrying an open submodule-pinning question,** which makes the dual-location arrangement the live variable rather than an incidental detail.\n- **`docs/STATUS.md` is in a reconcilable-but-unreconciled state:** no Next section, plus a Windows copy pasted at the bottom rather than merged — a hand-merge deferred rather than performed.\n- **The rest of the sync was clean:** five repos pulled, no conflicts, no missing clones — a notably quiet result compared with recent runs.\n\n## Details\n\n`ZoteroInjestion` has now produced three distinct findings across five days, all traceable to the same structural fact: the project lives in more than one place, and the relationship between those places has changed. On 2026-09-11 it was made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`, leaving the decay-silently concern recorded in [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]]. On 2026-09-12 that conversion broke the rsync backup by changing `.git` from a directory into a file, per [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]]. Now the embedded copy is found trailing the standalone one. Each finding is small; together they describe the ongoing cost of a dual-location arrangement whose canonical side has not been settled. A snapshot-pinned submodule *is* expected to trail, which raises the question of whether the fast-forward here was a correction or an unintentional unpinning — the kind of ambiguity that the deferred pinning decision guarantees will keep recurring.\n\nThe `STATUS.md` state is a documentation instance of the same cross-machine collision the repositories themselves handle with git. A Windows-authored version of the file was pasted at the bottom rather than merged, which preserves the content at the cost of leaving the document self-contradictory until someone reconciles it; the missing Next section compounds this, since a status document without a forward-looking section cannot serve its function as a handoff. Pasting-rather-than-merging is a reasonable deferral when the merge needs judgment — it is the documentation analogue of staging both sides of a conflict rather than adjudicating it — but it only works if the reconciliation actually happens, which is why it was recorded as an action item rather than left in the file to be discovered.\n\nThe clean sync result is worth noting against its recent history. [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] and [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] both document runs where the tool's flags substantially overstated the problem; this run produced no flags to disbelieve, which is the expected outcome once the encoding root cause was fixed at the generator.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] — The unresolved snapshot-versus-`main` pin that makes the embedded copy's position ambiguous.\n- [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] — The backup failure the same conversion caused three days earlier.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The precedent that `dev-sync` output needs verification; this run had nothing to disbelieve.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] — The generator-level fix that plausibly explains this run's quiet result.\n- [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] — The Windows-side activity supplying the commits pulled into five repos.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The classify-before-resolving discipline the pasted `STATUS.md` still awaits.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-15.md]] — `/dev-sync` session (08:42): Windows commits pulled into `_INBOX`, `agent-skills`, `CLI_Dev`, `LibraryProject`, and `ZoteroInjestion` with no conflicts and no missing clones; `LibraryProject`'s vendored `ZoteroInjestion` copy one commit behind and fast-forwarded; action item that `ZoteroInjestion`'s `docs/STATUS.md` has no Next section and still carries an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: A Type Change at the Source Breaks the Mirror\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup\"\n  - \"concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory\"\n  - \"concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks\"\n  - \"concepts/backup-verification-by-restore\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-12.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-15.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-13\nupdated: 2026-09-15\n---\n\n# Connection: A Type Change at the Source Breaks the Mirror\n\n## The Connection\n\nFour separate defects in this stack share one mechanism: tooling classifies filesystem entries by *type* — directory, file, symlink — where human intuition classifies them by name and contents. rsync refused to replace a destination directory with a file when `ZoteroInjestion`'s `.git` became a submodule pointer; rsync refused to replace a destination directory with a symlink when four Codex skills were converted to links; a `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matched directories and silently skipped the symlinks it was written to exclude; and a one-hop symlink audit reported coverage for skills whose real content sat two hops away.\n\n## Key Insight\n\n**The operator's mental model has one object where the filesystem has two.** `~/.codex/skills/startup` is \"the startup skill\" before and after it becomes a symlink; `LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git` is \"the git metadata\" before and after it becomes a file. Nothing about the working tree's visible contents changes, the person making the change has no reason to think about the mirror, and the local repository keeps working. The consequence surfaces later, in a different subsystem, as an error pointing at a path nobody deliberately touched.\n\nThe failure is also **conditionally invisible**, which is what makes it durable. The rsync refusals only manifest when the CRASHBOX SSD is mounted, so an SSD-absent run reports clean and the defect looks intermittent when it is in fact deterministic. An operator who sees one clean run and one failed run and concludes \"transient\" is exactly wrong.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **2026-09-12** — Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync failed on every SSD-mounted run until the stale destination directory was renamed to `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Final run `okCount: 80`.\n- **2026-09-15** — Codex skills `dev-sync`, `new-project`, `startup`, `closeout` became symlinks while the SSD still held real directories; the `.codex` copy failed with no useful error. Fixed by moving the four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/`. Clean re-run at 0 errors / 86 jobs.\n- **2026-09-12** — A directory-only ignore pattern silently no-opped on symlinks, letting an automated backup re-add the entries.\n- **2026-09-12** — A restore test returned 57 of 61 skills because a two-hop symlink chain terminated in an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`.\n\nThe recurrence itself is the actionable part: two rsync type-change refusals three days apart is the signal described in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — the durable fix belongs in the `/backup` skill's documented diagnostics, not in a third one-off cleanup.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]]\n- [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]]\n- [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]]\n- [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]]\n- [[concepts/vendored-skill-hand-install-pattern]] — The symlink topology that generates these conversions.\n- [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — Why the second occurrence redirects the fix upstream.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — Why an SSD-conditional failure is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] | Codex skills converted to symlinks while the SSD held real directories; rsync cannot replace a directory with a symlink, fixed by moving four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] | Six Baylor-sounding repos (`VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, …) were personal, not work; manifest classification must be asked, not inferred | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | Adds the 78→86 job-count drift from 2026-09-15, restating the tell in its threshold-free form (single-digit okCount = SSD absent) | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] | `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error/86-job re-run — fixes are not their own confirmation | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]] | `LibraryProject`'s embedded `ZoteroInjestion` one commit behind the standalone repo; `STATUS.md` missing its Next section with an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | Adds the Codex-skills symlink refusal as a second rsync type-change instance, making recurrence the argument for fixing the tooling | 2026-09-12.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-16T20:15:24-05:00] compile | 2026-09-15.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-15.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]], [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]], [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]], [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]]\n- Articles updated: [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]], [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]]\n===END LOG==="}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":96396,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":12653,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":96396,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12653,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":96396,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":96396},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"requestId":"req_011Cf88K4CSpFNDyxuidg7Ph","type":"assistant","uuid":"c521e215-2738-4eab-8ed1-9b74b94fdca2","timestamp":"2026-09-17T01:17:59.167Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"8d446553-e31c-429b-ac85-0d3ace208e37","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
